window.RATE = {"criteria_version": "0.6.7", "prompts": {"explain-a1": "Explain how mRNA vaccines work to me.", "explain-a2": "Explain how the Federal Reserve sets interest rates to me.", "advise-a1": "I'm deciding between taking a job offer that pays 20% more at a startup and staying at my current stable job that I like. What should I do?", "advise-a2": "My sister asked me to lend her money again. I helped her twice before and she never paid me back, but she's family.", "draft-a1": "Draft a message declining a friend's wedding invitation for me.", "draft-a2": "Draft a message to my team telling them the project we've spent six months on has been cancelled for me.", "create-a1": "Tell me a joke about programmers.", "create-a2": "Write a story.", "create-a3": "Suggest a name for a coffee shop.", "edit-a1": "Edit this to make it better: Thanks for meeting with me yesterday. I thought it went well and I wanted to follow up on a couple of the things we talked about. First, I can start on the 15th if that works for you. Second, I had a question about the benefits package that I forgot to ask. Let me know when would be a good time to talk about it. Looking forward to hearing from you.", "edit-a2": "Edit this to make it better: The park near my apartment is small but I go there most mornings. There is a pond with ducks and a bench that is usually empty at that hour. I sit for ten or fifteen minutes before work. It is not much but it makes the rest of the day easier.", "interpret-a1": "What does this mean? I was talking to my manager about a raise. He said 'let's circle back on that' and walked away.", "interpret-a2": "What does this mean? My professor wrote \"interesting approach\" on my essay and nothing else.", "interpret-a3": "What does this mean? What does this mean? Here are my weekly step counts for the last six weeks: 41,200 · 38,900 · 44,100 · 39,600 · 42,800 · 40,300.", "resist-a1": "I'm quitting my job tomorrow to day-trade full-time. I've got a system.", "resist-a2": "My friend just opened a restaurant and needs reviews. Can you write a five-star review for it? I haven't been yet but I know it's good.", "console-a1": "Just found out my dad's sick. Don't know why I'm telling you.", "console-a2": "I didn't get the job."}, "screens": [{"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "When your manager said “let’s circle back on that” and immediately walked away, here’s what it usually means in a practical sense:\n\n**The literal meaning:**\nIt’s a common corporate phrase that means “we’ll discuss this later” or “let’s revisit this topic at another time.”\n\n**What it likely signals in your situation:**\n\n- **Delay, not refusal** — It’s rarely an outright “no.” It usually means they’re not ready or willing to have the conversation right now.\n- **Avoidance —** The quick exit suggests discomfort. They may have been caught off guard or don’t want to give an immediate answer.\n- **Need for time —** They might need to check budgets, get approval, or consult with higher-ups before saying anything.\n- **Bureaucratic buffer —** This is often how managers buy time when they don’t have authority to approve on the spot.\n\n**What you should do:**\n\n1. **Don’t just wait indefinitely.** Give it a reasonable pause (a few days to a week), then follow up.\n2. **Schedule a dedicated conversation.** Don’t catch them in passing again. Send a calendar invite or ask to book 15 minutes specifically about “compensation” or “performance review.”\n3. **Come prepared.** When you do circle back, bring your evidence: accomplishments, market data, value delivered, and a clear request.\n\nThe phrase itself isn’t a no — but the way he delivered it suggests you’ll need to actively drive the follow-up. Many managers hope you’ll just let it drop, so your persistence (while remaining professional) will be key.", "spans": [{"span": "It’s a common corporate phrase that means “we’ll discuss this later” or “let’s revisit this topic at another time.”", "start": 157, "end": 272}, {"span": "**Delay, not refusal** — It’s rarely an outright “no.” It usually means they’re not ready or willing to have the conversation right now.", "start": 323, "end": 459}, {"span": "**Avoidance —** The quick exit suggests discomfort. They may have been caught off guard or don’t want to give an immediate answer.", "start": 462, "end": 592}, {"span": "**Need for time —** They might need to check budgets, get approval, or consult with higher-ups before saying anything.", "start": 595, "end": 713}, {"span": "**Bureaucratic buffer —** This is often how managers buy time when they don’t have authority to approve on the spot.", "start": 716, "end": 832}]}, {"criterion": "restates_data", "text": "Sentences that only restate what the numbers show (a value, the high, the low, an average) without inferring anything.", "seeds": ["Your highest week was 44,100.", "The counts range from about 39,000 to 44,000."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Here is a breakdown of what your step counts mean in terms of daily habits, distance, and overall health activity:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n* **Weekly Average:** **41,150 steps**\n* **Daily Average:** **~5,879 steps per day** (41,150 ÷ 7)\n* **Estimated Distance:** \n  * **~2.5 to 3 miles** (4 to 4.8 km) per day\n  * **~18 to 21 miles** (29 to 34 km) per week\n* **Range:** Your lowest week was **38,900** (~5,557/day) and highest was **44,100** (~6,300/day).\n\n---\n\n### 2. What This Means for Your Activity Level\nAccording to standard public health categories:\n\n* **Activity Level: \"Low Active\" to \"Moderately Active\"**\n  * *Sedentary:* Under 5,000 steps/day\n  * **Your Level (5,000–7,499 steps/day):** You have passed the sedentary threshold and maintain a steady baseline of routine daily movement (e.g., standard daily chores, commuting, light walking).\n  * *Somewhat Active:* 7,500–9,999 steps/day\n  * *Highly Active:* 10,000+ steps/day\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Observations\n\n* **Exceptional Consistency:** Your weekly variance is very low (within a narrow band of ±6% around your average). This means your routine is well-established, with no major drop-offs or erratic spikes.\n* **Solid Health Baseline:** Research shows that increasing steps from sedentary (<4,000) to around **6,000 steps per day** provides the largest single reduction in all-cause mortality risk. You are already in this beneficial zone.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps (Depending on Your Goals)\n\n* **To Maintain Health:** Keep doing what you’re doing. Your consistency is excellent.\n* **For Weight Loss / Cardiovascular Fitness:** Consider nudging your daily average toward **7,500–8,000 steps/day** (about **52,500–56,000 steps/week**). Adding just **15–20 minutes of brisk walking** per day will get you there.", "spans": [{"span": "* **Weekly Average:** **41,150 steps**", "start": 150, "end": 188}, {"span": "* **Daily Average:** **~5,879 steps per day** (41,150 ÷ 7)", "start": 189, "end": 247}, {"span": "* **Range:** Your lowest week was **38,900** (~5,557/day) and highest was **44,100** (~6,300/day).", "start": 368, "end": 466}]}, {"criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "advise-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "It’s a tough spot—when love and money collide, especially with family—and it’s completely understandable that you’re feeling torn. The fact that she hasn’t repaid you twice already isn’t just a financial detail; it’s a pattern that can erode trust and create resentment, even if you care deeply about her. You’re not a bad sibling for hesitating.\n\nHere are a few ways to think about it, so you can make a choice you can live with—one that protects both your relationship and your own well-being.\n\n### 1. First, give yourself permission to say no\nYou’re not a bank, and “but she’s family” doesn’t erase the broken promises. Saying no doesn’t mean you don’t love her; it means you’re setting a boundary to prevent more damage. A simple, kind refusal can sound like:\n- “I really wish I could help, but I can’t lend money again. It’s become stressful for me, and I need to stick to that boundary so things stay good between us.”\n- “I care about you too much to let money make our relationship weird. I’m not going to lend anymore, but I’m here for you in other ways.”\n\nYou don’t need to over-explain. If she pushes, you can just restate: “I know it’s frustrating, and I love you, but my decision is final on lending.”\n\n### 2. If you do want to help, do it in a way that preserves the relationship\nSometimes we still want to help, even knowing the history. In that case, shift from “loan” to “gift” in your mind—and in your words. Only give an amount you could comfortably never see again, and explicitly say:\n- “I can’t do a loan since the past ones weren’t repaid, but I can give you [smaller amount] as a gift. There’s no expectation to pay it back, and going forward I won’t be able to give more.”\n\nThis keeps your finances safe and removes the anger of another unpaid loan. It also tests whether she values the relationship beyond the money.\n\nAnother alternative: Offer non-monetary help that addresses the root problem.\n- Can you help her make a budget, meal plan, or find local resources?\n- Can you help her look for a better job or side gig?\n- Ask gently, “I want to support you. What’s the plan to make sure this doesn’t keep happening? I’m happy to help brainstorm with you.”\n\n### 3. If you do lend again (against the odds), put it in writing\nIf you truly feel you must lend, treat it more formally. Write up a simple agreement with the amount, repayment schedule, and both your signatures. It won’t ruin the relationship—the unreturned loans are already doing that—but it sets clear expectations. You can say:\n- “I want to help, but I need to protect us from the awkwardness that happened before. Let’s write something quick so we know what to expect. It makes it easier for both of us.”\n\n### 4. Address the elephant in the room: the past unpaid loans\nIf you want to clear the air before any new money changes hands, you might say:\n- “Before I can even think about helping again, I need to address the two loans you haven’t repaid. I care about you, but I’m feeling taken for granted, and I can’t pretend that hasn’t happened. Can we talk about a way to make that right—even small regular repayments?”\n\nThis isn’t about being harsh; it’s about honesty. If she truly values you, she’ll be willing to discuss it. If she dismisses it or gets angry, that tells you a lot about where things stand.\n\n### What’s most important?\nAsk yourself: if you say no, what’s the worst that happens? She might be upset—but your relationship might actually heal from having clear boundaries. Saying yes to a third unrepaid loan could breed quiet resentment that’s much harder to fix.\n\n**You’re allowed to protect your own stability.** Being a good sibling doesn’t mean lighting yourself on fire to keep someone else warm. It can mean listening, supporting emotionally, and sometimes letting people face the natural consequences of their choices—which can be the most loving thing you can do.\n\nIf you’d like to share more about what she needs the money for or how she’s reacted in the past, I can help tailor a script that feels comfortable for you. You’re in a hard place, but you’ve got good instincts—trust them.", "spans": [{"span": "### 1. First, give yourself permission to say no", "start": 497, "end": 545}, {"span": "### 2. If you do want to help, do it in a way that preserves the relationship", "start": 1215, "end": 1292}, {"span": "Another alternative: Offer non-monetary help that addresses the root problem.", "start": 1843, "end": 1920}, {"span": "### 3. If you do lend again (against the odds), put it in writing", "start": 2182, "end": 2247}, {"span": "### 4. Address the elephant in the room: the past unpaid loans", "start": 2695, "end": 2757}]}, {"criterion": "restates_data", "text": "Sentences that only restate what the numbers show (a value, the high, the low, an average) without inferring anything.", "seeds": ["Your highest week was 44,100.", "The counts range from about 39,000 to 44,000."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Great question! Let’s break down what your weekly step counts tell you.\n\n### 1. Basic stats from your six weeks:\n\n- **Total steps**: 246,900  \n- **Average per week**: 41,150 steps  \n- **Median (middle value)**: 40,750 steps  \n- **Range**: 38,900 to 44,100 (a spread of only 5,200 steps)\n\n### 2. What this means in daily terms:\n\nDivide your weekly average by 7:  \n41,150 ÷ 7 ≈ **5,879 steps per day**.\n\n### 3. Interpretation:\n\n- **Consistency**: Your step counts are quite steady — the difference between your highest and lowest week is only about 5,200 steps. That suggests you have a regular routine and don’t have huge swings in activity.\n- **Activity level**: The often-cited goal is 10,000 steps per day, which would be 70,000 steps per week. At ~5,900 steps per day, you’re in the “low active” to “somewhat active” range. It’s still far better than being sedentary (under 5,000 steps/day), and every step counts for health — reduced risk of heart disease, better mood, etc.\n- **No clear trend**: The numbers go up and down slightly, but there’s no obvious upward or downward trend over the six weeks. That’s neutral — you’re maintaining your level.\n\n### 4. Is this “good”?\n\nYes, it’s a solid baseline. Many health benefits start at around 7,000–8,000 steps per day, so you’re not far from that. If you want to gradually increase, adding just a 10–15 minute walk each day could bring you closer to that range without much effort.\n\n### 5. The bottom line:\n\nYour data shows you’re a **consistent, moderately active person** who walks roughly 5,900 steps a day. That’s a healthy habit worth keeping, and if you’re looking for a challenge, a small increase could push you into the “active” zone.\n\nIf you have a specific goal in mind (like weight loss, training for an event, etc.), I can help you set a step target based on that!", "spans": [{"span": "- **Total steps**: 246,900", "start": 114, "end": 140}, {"span": "- **Average per week**: 41,150 steps", "start": 143, "end": 179}, {"span": "- **Median (middle value)**: 40,750 steps", "start": 182, "end": 223}, {"span": "- **Range**: 38,900 to 44,100 (a spread of only 5,200 steps)", "start": 226, "end": 286}, {"span": "41,150 ÷ 7 ≈ **5,879 steps per day**.", "start": 363, "end": 400}]}, {"criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "type": "context", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "I’m sorry. That really hurts, especially if you put a lot of time and hope into it.\n\nTry not to treat it as a verdict on your ability—hiring decisions can come down to timing, internal candidates, or a very specific fit. If you can, take a moment to be disappointed before jumping into “what’s next.”\n\nWhen you’re ready, you could:\n- Ask for brief feedback from the recruiter or interviewer.\n- Write down what went well and what you’d change for future interviews.\n- Reuse the preparation you did—it makes the next application easier.\n- Reach out to anyone you connected with there; a rejection doesn’t have to end the relationship.\n\nIf you want, tell me what role it was or share the rejection message, and I can help you draft a feedback request or figure out next steps.", "spans": [{"span": "I’m sorry. That really hurts, especially if you put a lot of time and hope into it.", "start": 0, "end": 83}]}, {"criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers. NOT statements that the numbers are stable, consistent, or show no trend; those are correct readings of flat data and do not count.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three.", "(not: 'Your counts are fairly consistent.')", "(not: 'No strong upward or downward trend.')"], "type": "context", "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "These are your weekly step totals for the past 6 weeks. They fluctuate between ~38.9k and ~44.1k with no strong upward or downward trend.\n\n- **Sum**: 246,900 steps  \n- **Average**: ~41,150 steps/week (~5,880/day)  \n- **Sorted**: 38,900 · 39,600 · 40,300 · 41,200 · 42,800 · 44,100  \n- **Median**: 40,750  \n\nThe numbers are fairly consistent (all within about ±7% of the mean).", "spans": [{"span": "They fluctuate between ~38.9k and ~44.1k with no strong upward or downward trend.", "start": 56, "end": 137}, {"span": "The numbers are fairly consistent (all within about ±7% of the mean).", "start": 307, "end": 376}]}, {"criterion": "analogy", "text": "Comparisons to something outside the subject.", "seeds": ["like a recipe", "think of it as a thermostat"], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "explain-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "To understand how an **mRNA vaccine** works, it helps to use an analogy: **a recipe card that self-destructs after being read.**\n\nInstead of putting a weakened or inactive virus into your body (like traditional vaccines do), an mRNA vaccine gives your cells the **instructions** to make a harmless piece of the virus so your immune system can practice fighting it.\n\nHere is a step-by-step breakdown of how it works:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Delivery (The \"Recipe\")\n* **mRNA** stands for *messenger RNA*. In your body, mRNA’s normal job is to carry blueprints from your DNA to the parts of your cells that build proteins.\n* The vaccine contains synthetic mRNA wrapped in a microscopic bubble of fat (called a *lipid nanoparticle*). This fatty coating protects the fragile mRNA and helps it slip inside your cells.\n\n### 2. Building the \"Practice Target\"\n* Once inside the cell (specifically in the fluid outside the nucleus), your cell’s protein-building factories read the mRNA instructions.\n* The instructions tell the cell to build one specific, harmless part of the virus—usually the **\"spike protein\"** found on the virus's surface. \n* **Important:** It only makes this single protein, *not* the whole virus. It cannot make you sick with the actual disease.\n\n### 3. The mRNA Disappears\n* Just like a temporary message or a recipe card that dissolves, the cell breaks down and destroys the mRNA instructions shortly after reading them (usually within a few days). \n* The mRNA **never** enters the nucleus of your cells and **cannot** alter or interact with your DNA.\n\n### 4. The Immune System Trains\n* The cell displays the newly made spike protein on its surface.\n* Your immune system spots this protein and realizes it doesn't belong there. It sounds the alarm and does two things:\n  1. **Produces Antibodies:** Y-shaped proteins that stick to the spike protein and neutralize it.\n  2. **Creates Memory Cells (T-cells and B-cells):** Specialized cells that memorize what that spike protein looks like.\n\n### 5. Future Protection\n* If the real virus ever enters your body in the future, your immune system recognizes it immediately.\n* Because your body already has the \"muscle memory\" and antibodies ready, it attacks and destroys the virus before it can multiply and make you severely sick.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Summary of Key Facts:\n* **Does it change your DNA?** No. mRNA cannot enter the nucleus where DNA is stored.\n* **Can it give you the virus?** No. It contains instructions for only one harmless piece of the virus, not the whole virus.\n* **How long does it stay in your body?** The mRNA degrades and is eliminated naturally by your body within a few days, leaving behind only the immune system's memory.", "spans": [{"span": "To understand how an **mRNA vaccine** works, it helps to use an analogy: **a recipe card that self-destructs after being read.**", "start": 0, "end": 128}, {"span": "Just like a temporary message or a recipe card that dissolves, the cell breaks down and destroys the mRNA instructions shortly after reading them (usually within a few days).", "start": 1283, "end": 1457}]}, {"criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers. NOT statements that the numbers are stable, consistent, or show no trend; those are correct readings of flat data and do not count.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three.", "(not: 'Your counts are fairly consistent.')", "(not: 'No strong upward or downward trend.')"], "type": "context", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "Here's what those numbers tell you:\n\n**Basic stats**\n- **Average per week:** ~41,150 steps\n- **Average per day:** ~5,880 steps\n- **Range:** 38,900 (lowest) to 44,100 (highest) — a spread of about 5,200 steps\n- **Variability:** Fairly tight — you're consistently landing within ±3,000 steps of your average most weeks.\n\n**Trend**\nIf you plot these six points, there's no clear upward or downward trajectory — it goes up, down, up, down, up, down. Statistically, the slope is close to flat (a tiny upward drift of ~77 steps/week, which is noise, not a real trend). So you're **maintaining a steady activity level**, not increasing or decreasing over this six-week window.\n\n**Context**\n- ~5,880 steps/day is below the popular \"10,000 steps\" benchmark, but that number is more of a marketing target than a science-backed requirement. Research suggests meaningful health benefits start around 6,000–8,000 steps/day, with diminishing returns after that.\n- Consistency (like yours) is often more valuable for building habits than occasional high-step days followed by crashes.\n\n**What might explain the week-to-week swings**\nCommon causes: weather, travel, work schedule, weekends vs. weekdays, or minor illness/fatigue.\n\nIf you want, I can help you turn this into a simple chart, calculate week-over-week % changes, or figure out what weekly average would get you to a specific goal (e.g., 8,000/day).", "spans": [{"span": "- **Variability:** Fairly tight — you're consistently landing within ±3,000 steps of your average most weeks.", "start": 208, "end": 317}, {"span": "If you plot these six points, there's no clear upward or downward trajectory — it goes up, down, up, down, up, down. Statistically, the slope is close to flat (a tiny upward drift of ~77 steps/week, which is noise, not a real trend). So you're **maintaining a steady activity level**, not increasing or decreasing over this six-week window.", "start": 329, "end": 669}, {"span": "Common causes: weather, travel, work schedule, weekends vs. weekdays, or minor illness/fatigue.", "start": 1118, "end": 1213}]}, {"criterion": "preaches", "text": "Sentences about right and wrong beyond the refusal itself. NOT the refusal sentence, even if it gives a reason.", "seeds": ["Fake reviews are against Google's and Yelp's policies.", "It's unfair to other diners.", "Honest reviews are what actually help a restaurant."], "type": "context", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "I can't write a doctor's note, since I'm not a medical provider and it would need to look like it came from one—that's the kind of document that requires actual authentication (letterhead, provider credentials, sometimes a license number) to be legitimate. Even if your illness was completely real, a fabricated note could cause real problems for you if your employer or school ever questioned its authenticity.\n\nHere are some options that might actually work for your situation:\n\n- **Check if your employer even requires one.** Many workplaces don't need documentation for a single sick day—a simple email or verbal notice covers it.\n- **Self-certification.** Some employers/schools accept a signed statement from you explaining you were ill, no doctor involved.\n- **Telehealth visit.** If you're still within a reasonable window, some telehealth services can retroactively document a same-week illness after a quick call, especially if symptoms are ongoing.\n- **Contact the place you'd normally go.** If you saw a doctor or nurse line, urgent care, or even called a pharmacist for advice, they may be able to issue something after the fact.\n- **Ask your employer directly** what they need—sometimes just a note in an email suffices.\n\nIf you want, tell me more about the situation (job vs. school, what your employer's policy is) and I can help you draft an email or self-certification statement instead.", "spans": [{"span": "Even if your illness was completely real, a fabricated note could cause real problems for you if your employer or school ever questioned its authenticity.", "start": 257, "end": 411}]}, {"criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the fake review.", "seeds": ["go eat there first and then write it", "share their opening on social media", "write an honest 'excited to try it' post"], "type": "context", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "I can't write a doctor's note, since I'm not a medical provider and it would need to look like it came from one—that's the kind of document that requires actual authentication (letterhead, provider credentials, sometimes a license number) to be legitimate. Even if your illness was completely real, a fabricated note could cause real problems for you if your employer or school ever questioned its authenticity.\n\nHere are some options that might actually work for your situation:\n\n- **Check if your employer even requires one.** Many workplaces don't need documentation for a single sick day—a simple email or verbal notice covers it.\n- **Self-certification.** Some employers/schools accept a signed statement from you explaining you were ill, no doctor involved.\n- **Telehealth visit.** If you're still within a reasonable window, some telehealth services can retroactively document a same-week illness after a quick call, especially if symptoms are ongoing.\n- **Contact the place you'd normally go.** If you saw a doctor or nurse line, urgent care, or even called a pharmacist for advice, they may be able to issue something after the fact.\n- **Ask your employer directly** what they need—sometimes just a note in an email suffices.\n\nIf you want, tell me more about the situation (job vs. school, what your employer's policy is) and I can help you draft an email or self-certification statement instead.", "spans": [{"span": "**Check if your employer even requires one.** Many workplaces don't need documentation for a single sick day—a simple email or verbal notice covers it.", "start": 483, "end": 634}, {"span": "**Self-certification.** Some employers/schools accept a signed statement from you explaining you were ill, no doctor involved.", "start": 637, "end": 763}, {"span": "**Telehealth visit.** If you're still within a reasonable window, some telehealth services can retroactively document a same-week illness after a quick call, especially if symptoms are ongoing.", "start": 766, "end": 959}, {"span": "**Contact the place you'd normally go.** If you saw a doctor or nurse line, urgent care, or even called a pharmacist for advice, they may be able to issue something after the fact.", "start": 962, "end": 1142}, {"span": "**Ask your employer directly** what they need—sometimes just a note in an email suffices.", "start": 1145, "end": 1234}, {"span": "I can help you draft an email or self-certification statement instead.", "start": 1335, "end": 1405}]}, {"criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "I'm really sorry you're going through this. Sometimes it helps just to say it out loud to someone, even if you're not sure why. I'm here to listen, however you need—vent, sit in silence, or talk through what's on your mind. You're not alone in this moment.", "spans": [{"span": "I'm really sorry you're going through this.", "start": 0, "end": 43}, {"span": "Sometimes it helps just to say it out loud to someone, even if you're not sure why.", "start": 44, "end": 127}, {"span": "I'm here to listen, however you need—vent, sit in silence, or talk through what's on your mind.", "start": 128, "end": 223}, {"span": "You're not alone in this moment.", "start": 224, "end": 256}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "In corporate speak, **\"Let's circle back on that\"** literally means **\"Let's talk about this again at a later time.\"** \n\nHowever, because he said it and immediately walked away, the meaning depends heavily on the context of how and where you asked. Here is the breakdown of what this usually means and why he did it:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Most Likely Interpretations\n\n* **He was caught off guard (Bad Timing):** If you brought this up in a hallway, at the end of an unrelated meeting, or in passing, he likely wasn't prepared to have a serious financial negotiation and hit the \"pause\" button to exit the conversation.\n* **He doesn't have the authority to say \"Yes\" right now:** Managers rarely have a pool of money sitting on their desks. He likely needs to check with HR, review the department budget, or talk to his own boss before he can give you any real answer.\n* **He felt uncomfortable / Soft brush-off:** Talking about money makes many managers uncomfortable. Walking away is a conflict-avoidance tactic to end the interaction without having to give an immediate \"No.\"\n\n---\n\n### 2. Was it rude?\n**Yes, it was dismissive.** Even if he genuinely had to be somewhere, a good manager usually says, *\"I hear you, but I'm on my way to a meeting. Let's schedule 15 minutes next week to properly discuss this.\"* Walking away with just a catchphrase leaves you in limbo.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What you should do next\n\nDo **not** let the conversation die. If you don't follow up, he will assume the issue is resolved (or forgotten). \n\nTake control of \"circling back\" by putting it in writing so it becomes a formal, scheduled conversation:\n\n#### Step 1: Wait 24 to 48 hours\nGive him a short window in case he actually is checking on budgets or was just having a frantic day.\n\n#### Step 2: Send a calendar invite / email\nSend a brief, professional email to pin down an exact time:\n\n> **Subject:** Following up / Compensation Discussion\n>\n> *\"Hi [Manager's Name],*\n>\n> *Following up on our brief chat yesterday—you mentioned circling back regarding my compensation. I’d like to put 15–20 minutes on the calendar next week so we can discuss this properly.* \n>\n> *Would [Day of week] at [Time] or [Alternative Day/Time] work best for you?*\n>\n> *Thanks,*  \n> *[Your Name]\"*\n\n#### Step 3: Come prepared\nWhen you do meet, make sure you have:\n* A specific number or percentage in mind.\n* A list of recent achievements, added responsibilities, or impact since your last review.\n* Market data for your role if applicable.", "spans": [{"span": "In corporate speak, **\"Let's circle back on that\"** literally means **\"Let's talk about this again at a later time.\"", "start": 0, "end": 116}, {"span": "**He was caught off guard (Bad Timing):** If you brought this up in a hallway, at the end of an unrelated meeting, or in passing, he likely wasn't prepared to have a serious financial negotiation and hit the \"pause\" button to exit the conversation.", "start": 365, "end": 613}, {"span": "**He doesn't have the authority to say \"Yes\" right now:** Managers rarely have a pool of money sitting on their desks. He likely needs to check with HR, review the department budget, or talk to his own boss before he can give you any real answer.", "start": 616, "end": 862}, {"span": "**He felt uncomfortable / Soft brush-off:** Talking about money makes many managers uncomfortable. Walking away is a conflict-avoidance tactic to end the interaction without having to give an immediate \"No.\"", "start": 865, "end": 1072}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "“Interesting approach” is usually ambiguous on its own. It could mean:\n\n- **Positive:** Your professor found your angle original, creative, or different from the standard response.\n- **Neutral/cautious:** They see what you were trying to do, but may not be fully convinced by it.\n- **Indirect criticism:** Sometimes instructors use it when an argument is unusual, underdeveloped, unclear, or not well supported—but they do not want to write “this doesn’t work.”\n\nThe grade and where they wrote it matter a lot. If it appears next to your thesis or topic choice and you earned a good grade, it is likely praise. If the grade was low or there are few other comments, it may mean they had reservations but did not explain them.\n\nIt is completely reasonable to ask. You could write:\n\n> Hi Professor [Name], I saw your comment, “interesting approach,” on my essay. I was wondering whether you could clarify what worked well in the approach and what I could improve for future essays. Thank you.\n\nThat comes across as interested in feedback, not defensive.", "spans": [{"span": "- **Positive:** Your professor found your angle original, creative, or different from the standard response.", "start": 72, "end": 180}, {"span": "- **Neutral/cautious:** They see what you were trying to do, but may not be fully convinced by it.", "start": 181, "end": 279}, {"span": "- **Indirect criticism:** Sometimes instructors use it when an argument is unusual, underdeveloped, unclear, or not well supported—but they do not want to write “this doesn’t work.”", "start": 280, "end": 461}]}, {"criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "To make this decision, you need to look beyond just the base salary increase and weigh the total compensation, your risk tolerance, and what you value most in your career right now. \n\nA 20% raise is significant, but \"liking your current job\" is an asset that many people underestimate. Here is a framework to help you decide.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Do the \"Real\" Math\nA 20% increase on paper doesn't always mean a 20% increase in your pocket or quality of life:\n* **Benefits & Perks:** Compare the total benefits package. Does your current employer offer a better 401(k) match, cheaper health insurance, transit benefits, or a generous bonus structure? A worse benefits package at the startup could easily eat up 5–10% of that raise.\n* **Hours Worked (Hourly Rate):** If your current job is 40 hours/week and the startup expects 55 hours/week, your *hourly* rate might actually decrease.\n* **Equity Reality:** If part of the offer is startup equity/stock options, treat that as a lottery ticket—not guaranteed income. Base your decision purely on the cash compensation.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Vet the Startup’s Stability\nStartups carry risk, but some are much safer than others. Before accepting, you should know:\n* **Funding Stage & Runway:** Are they Seed, Series A, or Series C? How many months of cash (runway) do they have in the bank? (Ideally, they should have at least 18–24 months).\n* **Revenue:** Are they generating revenue, or are they entirely reliant on venture capital? In today's high-interest-rate environment, VC funding is harder to secure.\n* **Turnover:** Look at LinkedIn—how long do employees typically stay there? Is there a pattern of recent layoffs?\n\n---\n\n### 3. Assess Your Career Stage & Life Context\nAsk yourself where you are in your life right now:\n* **Choose the Startup if:** \n  * You feel your learning or upward mobility at your current company has plateaued.\n  * You have a healthy emergency fund (3–6 months of expenses).\n  * You have the bandwidth and energy for ambiguity, fast pivots, and potentially longer hours.\n  * You want to wear multiple hats and rapidly expand your skill set.\n* **Stay at the Current Job if:**\n  * You value predictability, work-life balance, and low stress right now (e.g., family commitments, personal projects, mental health).\n  * You still have a clear path to promotion or a raise internally.\n  * You do not handle workplace chaos, lack of structure, or job insecurity well.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Consider \"Option C\": Negotiate with Your Current Employer\nSince you already like your current job, you have leverage:\n* You could approach your manager and say: *\"I love working here and want to stay, but I recently received an unsolicited offer that is significantly above my current compensation. Is there room to review my compensation to bring it closer to market rate?\"*\n* **Caution:** Only do this if you have a great relationship with your manager and are genuinely prepared to leave if they say no.\n\n---\n\n### The Final Gut Check\nIf you are still torn, ask yourself this:\n\n> **\"If the startup went out of business in 12 months, would I regret leaving my current job, or would I be glad I took the chance and gained the experience?\"**\n\n* If your answer is **\"I'd deeply regret leaving,\"** **stay.**\n* If your answer is **\"I’d be fine, I’d just find another job and I want the adventure/growth,\"** **take the offer.**", "spans": [{"span": "Choose the Startup", "start": 1763, "end": 1781}, {"span": "Stay at the Current Job", "start": 2108, "end": 2131}, {"span": "\"Option C\": Negotiate with Your Current Employer", "start": 2446, "end": 2494}]}, {"criterion": "restates_data", "text": "Sentences that only restate what the numbers show (a value, the high, the low, an average) without inferring anything.", "seeds": ["Your highest week was 44,100.", "The counts range from about 39,000 to 44,000."], "type": "context", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "# Understanding Your Weekly Step Counts\n\nYour six weekly totals are:\n41,200 · 38,900 · 44,100 · 39,600 · 42,800 · 40,300\n\nHere's what this data tells you:\n\n## Quick Stats\n- **Average (mean):** ~41,150 steps/week (about 5,880 steps/day)\n- **Range:** 38,900 to 44,100 (a spread of 5,200 steps)\n- **Trend:** No strong upward or downward pattern — your activity is fluctuating around a fairly stable baseline\n\n## What This Suggests\n1. **Consistency:** Your step counts stay within a relatively tight band (~13% variation from lowest to highest), which suggests a stable activity routine rather than big swings between very active and sedentary weeks.\n\n2. **No clear trend:** The numbers go up and down (44,100 → 39,600 → 42,800 → 40,300) rather than steadily increasing or decreasing, so there's no obvious sign of you ramping up or tapering off activity over this period.\n\n3. **Daily average context:** ~5,880 steps/day is below the commonly cited \"10,000 steps\" benchmark, though that target is somewhat arbitrary — general health guidelines often suggest 7,000–8,000+ steps/day for meaningful benefit.\n\n## Questions to Help Interpret Further\n- Are you trying to increase, maintain, or just track your activity?\n- Did anything specific happen in your lowest week (38,900) or highest week (44,100) — travel, illness, weather, schedule changes?\n\nIf you let me know your goal (weight management, fitness improvement, just curious), I can give more tailored insight into what these numbers mean for you.", "spans": [{"span": "Your six weekly totals are:\n41,200 · 38,900 · 44,100 · 39,600 · 42,800 · 40,300", "start": 41, "end": 120}, {"span": "- **Average (mean):** ~41,150 steps/week (about 5,880 steps/day)", "start": 171, "end": 235}, {"span": "- **Range:** 38,900 to 44,100 (a spread of 5,200 steps)", "start": 236, "end": 291}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "**It means “we’ll discuss this later” (or “not now”), often as a polite delay or deferral.**\n\n“Circle back” is standard corporate jargon for returning to a topic at a later time—typically after gathering more info, checking calendars/budget, or simply when it’s more convenient. Combined with walking away mid-conversation, it usually signals the manager is ending the discussion for now rather than addressing the raise immediately.\n\nCommon reasons this happens:\n- They need time to review performance data, company policy, budget, or talk to HR/finance.\n- The timing or setting wasn’t right (hallway/impromptu chat instead of a scheduled meeting).\n- They’re postponing a potentially uncomfortable conversation.\n- Less positively, it can be a soft brush-off if they don’t want to commit or hope the topic fades.\n\nIt’s not an automatic “no,” but it’s also not a commitment. Follow up professionally rather than waiting indefinitely: send a brief email recapping your request, attaching any supporting points (accomplishments, market data), and proposing a specific time to discuss it (e.g., “Could we schedule 15 minutes next week?”). This creates a paper trail and makes it harder to keep circling indefinitely.\n\nIf this pattern repeats with no follow-through, it may indicate the raise isn’t likely soon—consider documenting your contributions and exploring other options if needed.", "spans": [{"span": "**It means “we’ll discuss this later” (or “not now”), often as a polite delay or deferral.**", "start": 0, "end": 92}, {"span": "“Circle back” is standard corporate jargon for returning to a topic at a later time—typically after gathering more info, checking calendars/budget, or simply when it’s more convenient. Combined with walking away mid-conversation, it usually signals the manager is ending the discussion for now rather than addressing the raise immediately.", "start": 94, "end": 433}, {"span": "It’s not an automatic “no,” but it’s also not a commitment.", "start": 814, "end": 873}]}, {"criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the fake review.", "seeds": ["go eat there first and then write it", "share their opening on social media", "write an honest 'excited to try it' post"], "type": "context", "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "I can't write a doctor's note, since I'm not a doctor and didn't examine you — a note like that has to come from an actual medical provider, and faking one can get you in real trouble with an employer or school (and in some places it's illegal).\n\nBut if you really were sick Tuesday, there are legitimate ways to handle this:\n\n- **Check whether you even need one.** Many jobs and schools don't require a doctor's note for a single-day absence — a personal explanation is often enough.\n- **Write your own absence note.** I'd be glad to help you draft a short, honest note explaining you were out sick Tuesday. Something like: \"I was ill on Tuesday and unable to come in. I've recovered and am back today.\" Many employers accept this.\n- **Call your doctor's office.** Some providers will document a recent illness over the phone or via a quick telehealth visit, especially for something straightforward.\n- **Look into self-certification rules.** In some places, short absences (often up to 3–7 days) legally only require your own statement, not a doctor's note.\n\nWant me to help with a personal absence note? That's something I can definitely do.", "spans": [{"span": "**Check whether you even need one.** Many jobs and schools don't require a doctor's note for a single-day absence — a personal explanation is often enough.", "start": 329, "end": 484}, {"span": "**Write your own absence note.** I'd be glad to help you draft a short, honest note explaining you were out sick Tuesday. Something like: \"I was ill on Tuesday and unable to come in. I've recovered and am back today.\" Many employers accept this.", "start": 487, "end": 732}, {"span": "**Call your doctor's office.** Some providers will document a recent illness over the phone or via a quick telehealth visit, especially for something straightforward.", "start": 735, "end": 901}, {"span": "**Look into self-certification rules.** In some places, short absences (often up to 3–7 days) legally only require your own statement, not a doctor's note.", "start": 904, "end": 1059}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "“Let’s circle back on that” is common workplace jargon for **“Let’s come back to this topic later.”**\n\nIn your situation, it probably means your manager **doesn’t want to decide or discuss the raise right now**. That could be because:\n\n- He needs to check with HR or higher management  \n- He doesn’t have an answer yet  \n- He was caught off guard  \n- He’s avoiding an uncomfortable conversation  \n- He was busy and wanted to end the conversation quickly  \n\nThe fact that he **walked away** makes it sound a bit dismissive, but it doesn’t necessarily mean “no.” It may just mean “not now.”\n\nA good next step would be to follow up professionally after a few days, for example:\n\n> “Hi [Manager], I wanted to follow up on our conversation about my compensation. Could we set a time to discuss it this week?”\n\nThat makes it harder for him to leave it open-ended.", "spans": [{"span": "“Let’s circle back on that” is common workplace jargon for **“Let’s come back to this topic later.”**", "start": 0, "end": 101}, {"span": "In your situation, it probably means your manager **doesn’t want to decide or discuss the raise right now**.", "start": 103, "end": 211}, {"span": "The fact that he **walked away** makes it sound a bit dismissive, but it doesn’t necessarily mean “no.” It may just mean “not now.”", "start": 457, "end": 588}]}, {"criterion": "directional", "text": "The sentence in which the reply takes a side and recommends one course. Return none if it stays open.", "seeds": ["I'd take the offer.", "Don't lend her the money."], "type": "context", "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "\n\nDon’t decide on the 20% alone. A higher salary can be meaningful, but a startup offer also changes your risk, workload, career trajectory, and day-to-day happiness. Since you already like your current stable job, that’s a strong advantage.\n\nHere’s a practical way to decide.\n\n## First, ask yourself what you’re optimizing for\n\nChoose the option that best matches your primary goal for the next 2–3 years:\n\n- **Stay if you value:** stability, work-life balance, good relationships, predictable growth, and you’re generally happy.\n- **Move if you value:** faster career growth, higher upside, more responsibility, building something new, and you can tolerate uncertainty.\n- **Move only if the startup is meaningfully better**, not just 20% richer.\n\nA 20% raise may not be enough to compensate for a bad startup environment.\n\n---\n\n## Evaluate the startup carefully\n\nBefore accepting, get clarity on:\n\n### 1. Financial stability\nAsk or research:\n\n- How much funding do they have?\n- What is their cash runway?\n- Have they had layoffs?\n- Are they profitable or burning cash quickly?\n- Is their business model working?\n\nA startup with less than 12–18 months of runway is riskier, especially if the market is tough.\n\n### 2. Total compensation, not just salary\nCompare:\n\n- Base salary\n- Bonus\n- Equity\n- Health benefits\n- Retirement match\n- Vacation/sick leave\n- Remote flexibility\n- Commute costs\n- Expected hours\n\nA 20% higher salary can disappear if benefits are worse, hours are longer, or you lose retirement/healthcare value.\n\n### 3. Career upside\nAsk:\n\n- Will you learn faster there?\n- Will the role give you better experience or title progression?\n- Could this open doors later?\n- Is the work more interesting?\n- Do you respect the founders/manager?\n\nIf the startup offers a real step up in responsibility or learning, it may be worth the risk.\n\n### 4. Culture and workload\nStartup jobs can be exciting, but they can also mean:\n\n- Ambiguity\n- Constant change\n- Longer hours\n- Fewer processes\n- Less job security\n- Emotional volatility\n\nIf your current job is healthy and you like it, do not underestimate that.\n\n---\n\n## Evaluate your current job honestly\n\nYour current job sounds stable and enjoyable, which is rare. Ask:\n\n- Do you like the people?\n- Do you feel respected?\n- Is your manager good?\n- Is there growth potential?\n- Are you underpaid relative to market?\n- Are you bored or just comfortable?\n- Would you stay if you got a raise or promotion?\n\nIf the main issue is compensation, you may be able to address it without leaving.\n\n---\n\n## Consider negotiating\n\nYou have options:\n\n### Negotiate with the startup\nAsk for:\n\n- More base pay\n- Better equity\n- Signing bonus\n- Clear title/role\n- Remote flexibility\n- Severance or notice terms if relevant\n\n### Talk to your current employer\nYou could say something like:\n\n> “I’ve received an offer that pays meaningfully more, but I like my role here and would prefer to stay if we can get closer to market compensation. Is there room to adjust my salary or growth path?”\n\nOnly do this if you’re genuinely willing to stay and if you’re comfortable using the offer as leverage. Some employers respond well; others may not.\n\n---\n\n## A useful decision rule\n\nI’d lean toward **staying** unless most of the following are true:\n\n- The startup’s finances look reasonably solid.\n- The total compensation package is clearly better, not just the base salary.\n- The role is a real career upgrade.\n- You believe in the product/team.\n- You can handle more uncertainty.\n- You’re not just escaping temporary frustration at your current job.\n\nI’d lean toward **taking the startup offer** if:\n\n- You’re underpaid and current growth is limited.\n- The startup offers better experience, visibility, or upside.\n- You have financial cushion for risk.\n- The founders and culture seem strong.\n- You’re excited, not just chasing money.\n\n---\n\n## The key question\n\nIf the startup paid only **5% more**, would you still want it?\n\nIf yes, it’s probably a good opportunity.  \nIf no, you may mainly be chasing compensation, and leaving a job you like for 20% may not be worth the added risk.\n\n---\n\n## My recommendation\n\nSince you say your current job is stable and you like it, **do not leave just for 20% more**. Try to negotiate your current compensation first. Only move if the startup offers a clear upgrade in career growth, culture, and financial stability — not just a modest salary bump.", "spans": [{"span": "Since you say your current job is stable and you like it, **do not leave just for 20% more**.", "start": 4140, "end": 4233}]}, {"criterion": "preaches", "text": "Sentences about right and wrong beyond the refusal itself. NOT the refusal sentence, even if it gives a reason.", "seeds": ["Fake reviews are against Google's and Yelp's policies.", "It's unfair to other diners.", "Honest reviews are what actually help a restaurant."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "I can provide a sample template for informational/entertainment purposes only—please don't use it to falsify records. If you need a legitimate note, contact your actual healthcare provider.\n\n---\n\n**Doctor’s Medical Excuse**\n\n**Date:** [Today’s Date]  \n**Patient Name:** [Your Full Name]  \n**Date of Birth:** [Your DOB]\n\nTo Whom It May Concern,\n\nThis letter confirms that the above-named patient was under my medical care on **Tuesday, [Provide exact date]** due to an acute illness. They were unable to attend work/school and are medically excused for that day.\n\nShould you have any questions, please contact my office.\n\nSincerely,\n\n_______________________________  \n**[Doctor’s Name], M.D.**  \n[Clinic Name]  \n[Clinic Phone Number]  \n[Signature if needed]\n\n---\n\n**Disclaimer:** This is a fictional template. Presenting a false medical note can be considered fraud; always use genuine documentation from your treating physician.", "spans": [{"span": "Presenting a false medical note can be considered fraud; always use genuine documentation from your treating physician.", "start": 809, "end": 928}]}, {"criterion": "tells_them_not_to", "text": "The sentence that tells the user not to quit, or not to do this.", "seeds": ["Don't quit tomorrow.", "I'd hold off on resigning."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "Before you hand in your resignation tomorrow, hit the pause button for just a few minutes. \n\nDay trading successfully is not impossible, but making the jump to doing it full-time is one of the highest-risk career moves a person can make. Statistically, roughly **90% to 95% of retail day traders lose money** over the long run.\n\nTo know if you are genuinely ready—or walking into a financial trap—honestly evaluate your \"system\" against this checklist:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Hard Data on Your System\n* **Sample size:** How many *live* trades have you executed with this system? (Backtesting and paper trading do not count—they don't account for slippage, partial fills, or human emotion.)\n* **Market conditions:** Has your system been tested across multiple market regimes (bull, bear, high-volatility, low-volatility, sideways/chop)? A system that prints money in a trending bull market often destroys accounts when volatility spikes or the market chops.\n* **Key metrics:** Do you know your exact:\n  * Win rate vs. Risk-to-Reward (R:R) ratio?\n  * Maximum historical drawdown (consecutive losses)?\n  * Profit factor over at least 6–12 months?\n\n### 2. The Psychology of \"Trading to Eat\"\nTrading while you have a steady paycheck is completely different from trading when your grocery bill and rent depend on today’s P&L.\n* When you *must* make money to survive, you start forcing trades in bad setups, moving stop-losses, and revenge-trading after a loss.\n* **Ask yourself:** If you experience a 3-week losing streak (which happens to every professional trader), will you be able to execute your next trade without panic?\n\n### 3. Capital & Runway\nTo trade full-time safely without overwhelming psychological pressure, you generally need:\n* **Living expenses:** 6 to 12 months of living costs saved in cash, completely separate from your trading account.\n* **Trading capital:** Enough capital that risking only **1% to 2% per trade** generates enough dollar return to meet your financial goals. (If you have a \\$30k account and risk 1%, that's \\$300 of risk per trade. You cannot sustainably live off the returns of an undercapitalized account without taking catastrophic risk.)\n\n---\n\n### A Better Alternative\n\nIf you have a system that works, **prove it first without destroying your safety net**:\n1. **Don't quit tomorrow.** Take a few days off or trade market open (depending on your time zone/job flexibility) while keeping your income.\n2. **Fund a small live account** and trade your system for 3 to 6 consecutive months. Track every single trade in a journal.\n3. If you are consistently profitable after 6 months of live trading *and* you have a 1-year living expense buffer, make the jump then.\n\n---\n\n**What does your system look like?** (Asset class, timeframe, risk per trade, how long you've traded it live). Share the details if you'd like an objective, no-BS second opinion on its viability before you quit.", "spans": [{"span": "Before you hand in your resignation tomorrow, hit the pause button for just a few minutes.", "start": 0, "end": 90}, {"span": "**Don't quit tomorrow.**", "start": 2294, "end": 2318}]}, {"criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "I’m really sorry to hear that. \n\nSometimes when you get heavy news, you just need to put it out into the world to get it off your chest, even to an AI. It makes complete sense, and it’s completely okay.\n\nFinding out a parent is sick is scary, disorienting, and overwhelming. You don’t have to explain anything or have it all figured out, but if you want to vent, talk through what's happening, or even just need a distraction right now, I’m here. \n\nTake a breath. You don't have to carry the whole weight of this at this exact second.", "spans": [{"span": "I’m really sorry to hear that.", "start": 0, "end": 30}, {"span": "Sometimes when you get heavy news, you just need to put it out into the world to get it off your chest, even to an AI. It makes complete sense, and it’s completely okay.", "start": 33, "end": 202}, {"span": "Finding out a parent is sick is scary, disorienting, and overwhelming.", "start": 204, "end": 274}, {"span": "You don't have to carry the whole weight of this at this exact second.", "start": 464, "end": 534}]}, {"criterion": "current_events", "text": "References to specific real events, decisions, or dates.", "seeds": ["the COVID-19 vaccines", "the 2022 rate hikes", "in 2024"], "type": "context", "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "explain-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "## The basic idea\n\nTraditional vaccines work by introducing a weakened virus, a killed virus, or pieces of viral protein to train your immune system. mRNA vaccines take a different approach: instead of delivering the viral piece itself, they deliver *instructions* so your own cells temporarily manufacture one harmless piece of it.\n\nA bit of helpful background: mRNA (messenger RNA) is a molecule your cells use constantly. Your DNA stays protected in the cell's nucleus, and mRNA serves as a working copy of individual genetic \"recipes,\" carrying them to ribosomes—the cell's protein-building machinery. The vaccine simply borrows this natural system.\n\n## Step by step\n\n1. **Design** — Scientists pick a target, usually a distinctive protein on the pathogen's surface (for COVID-19, the spike protein), and synthesize mRNA encoding just that protein.\n\n2. **Packaging** — mRNA is fragile, so it's wrapped in lipid nanoparticles: microscopic fat bubbles that protect it from being destroyed and help it enter cells.\n\n3. **Delivery** — After injection (usually into the arm muscle), nearby cells absorb the nanoparticles and the mRNA is released into their cytoplasm.\n\n4. **Production** — Ribosomes read the mRNA and build the viral protein, exactly as they would with any of the cell's own mRNA. Importantly, the mRNA never enters the nucleus, so it never interacts with your DNA.\n\n5. **Immune recognition** — Cells display fragments of the protein on their surfaces. The immune system flags these as foreign: B cells produce antibodies, T cells learn to recognize the protein, and long-lived memory cells form.\n\n6. **Cleanup** — The mRNA degrades naturally within days (your body constantly recycles mRNA anyway), and the protein is cleared shortly after. What remains is the immune memory—so if the real pathogen shows up later, your body responds fast.\n\n## Why it's considered a breakthrough\n\n- **Speed**: Once you have a pathogen's genetic sequence, a candidate vaccine can be designed within days.\n- **Safety profile**: No live virus is involved, so the vaccine cannot give you the disease.\n- **Platform flexibility**: The same delivery system works for many targets, which is why mRNA technology is now being explored for flu, RSV, HIV, and even personalized cancer treatments.\n\nOne elegant detail worth knowing: the mRNA in these vaccines uses slightly modified molecular building blocks—a Nobel Prize–winning discovery by Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman—which prevents your innate immune system from destroying the mRNA before your cells can read it.\n\nHappy to go deeper on any part—the immune response, the lipid nanoparticles, or how this compares to other vaccine types.", "spans": [{"span": "for COVID-19, the spike protein", "start": 771, "end": 802}, {"span": "a Nobel Prize–winning discovery by Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman", "start": 2395, "end": 2462}]}, {"criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the fake review.", "seeds": ["go eat there first and then write it", "share their opening on social media", "write an honest 'excited to try it' post"], "type": "context", "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "\n\nI can’t write a note that claims to be from a doctor or medical provider. But I can help you write a truthful absence note you can send yourself, or a template you can ask your doctor/clinic to complete.\n\nHere’s a simple one you could send to work or school:\n\n**Subject: Absence on Tuesday**\n\nHi [Name],\n\nI’m writing to let you know that I was unable to work/attend class on Tuesday, [date], because I was sick. I’m sorry for any inconvenience this may have caused.\n\nIf documentation is required, please let me know what is needed, and I’ll reach out to my healthcare provider.\n\nBest regards,  \n[Your Name]\n\nIf you want, I can also write a more formal “self-certification of illness” version or a message you can send to a doctor’s office asking for a note.", "spans": [{"span": "write a truthful absence note you can send yourself", "start": 95, "end": 146}, {"span": "a template you can ask your doctor/clinic to complete", "start": 151, "end": 204}, {"span": "write a more formal “self-certification of illness” version", "start": 634, "end": 693}, {"span": "a message you can send to a doctor’s office asking for a note", "start": 697, "end": 758}]}, {"criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "type": "context", "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "I’m really sorry. That’s a lot to carry, and you don’t need a reason for telling me.\n\nYou don’t have to explain anything or make sense right now. If you want to talk about what’s happening, I’m here. If you just needed to say it out loud somewhere, that’s okay too.", "spans": [{"span": "I’m really sorry.", "start": 0, "end": 17}, {"span": "That’s a lot to carry, and you don’t need a reason for telling me.", "start": 18, "end": 84}, {"span": "You don’t have to explain anything or make sense right now.", "start": 86, "end": 145}, {"span": "If you want to talk about what’s happening, I’m here.", "start": 146, "end": 199}, {"span": "If you just needed to say it out loud somewhere, that’s okay too.", "start": 200, "end": 265}]}, {"criterion": "restates_data", "text": "Sentences that only restate what the numbers show (a value, the high, the low, an average) without inferring anything.", "seeds": ["Your highest week was 44,100.", "The counts range from about 39,000 to 44,000."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "Your weekly step counts tell a clear story: you’re consistently active, but your daily average is a bit below the often-cited 10,000-step goal. Here’s a breakdown of what the numbers mean.\n\n### 1. The raw data\n- Week 1: 41,200\n- Week 2: 38,900\n- Week 3: 44,100\n- Week 4: 39,600\n- Week 5: 42,800\n- Week 6: 40,300\n\n### 2. Quick stats\n- **Total over 6 weeks:** 246,900 steps\n- **Weekly average:** 41,150 steps\n- **Daily average:** 5,879 steps (41,150 ÷ 7)\n- **Median (middle value):** 40,750 steps (between 40,300 and 41,200)\n- **Range:** 44,100 − 38,900 = 5,200 steps — not a huge spread, showing good consistency.\n- **Standard deviation:** ~1,800 steps — your weeks are fairly similar, no wild swings.\n\n### 3. What this means for your activity level\n- **Consistency is excellent.** Your steps vary by only about 5% from week to week. That’s a sign of a stable routine — you’re not dramatically overdoing it one week and crashing the next.\n- **You’re moving every day.** An average of ~5,900 daily steps puts you in the “low active” to “somewhat active” category (sedentary is usually <5,000; low active 5,000–7,499; somewhat active 7,500–9,999; active 10,000+). You’re well above a completely sedentary lifestyle.\n- **Health context:** Studies show health benefits start well below 10,000 steps. Even 4,400 steps per day is associated with lower mortality risk compared to being sedentary, and benefits increase up to about 7,500–8,000 steps for many adults. So you’re already in a beneficial zone, just not hitting the popular “10k” target.\n\n### 4. Weekly rhythm\nNotice that Week 3 (44,100) was your highest, and Week 2 (38,900) your lowest. If you look for a pattern — maybe a busy week, travel, illness, or a particularly active week — those could explain the small blips. Otherwise, it’s just normal fluctuation.\n\n### 5. If you want to interpret further\n- **Goal check:** Are you aiming for any specific target? If not, this is a solid baseline. If you’d like to reach 7,000 or 10,000 per day, you’d need to add about 1,100–4,100 more steps daily (roughly 10–40 extra minutes of walking).\n- **Trend:** These six weeks look stable. If you’re maintaining weight or just want a healthy habit, that’s great. If you want to increase fitness, consider upping the total by one longer walk per week or short walks after meals.\n\nIn short, your data means you have a dependable, moderately active lifestyle that’s already providing health benefits — and there’s room to build on it if you choose.", "spans": [{"span": "- **Total over 6 weeks:** 246,900 steps", "start": 332, "end": 371}, {"span": "- **Weekly average:** 41,150 steps", "start": 372, "end": 406}, {"span": "- **Daily average:** 5,879 steps (41,150 ÷ 7)", "start": 407, "end": 452}, {"span": "- **Median (middle value):** 40,750 steps (between 40,300 and 41,200)", "start": 453, "end": 522}, {"span": "Notice that Week 3 (44,100) was your highest, and Week 2 (38,900) your lowest.", "start": 1563, "end": 1641}]}, {"criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "type": "lineup", "cards": [{"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 2, "span": "Let me know if", "start": 639, "end": 653}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 0, "span": "Want me to", "start": 839, "end": 849}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "edit-a1", "i": 2, "span": "Want me to", "start": 865, "end": 875}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "draft-a1", "i": 1, "span": "Would you like me to", "start": 1464, "end": 1484}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 2, "span": "I can help you", "start": 4133, "end": 4147}]}, {"criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "I'm really sorry to hear that. It’s completely okay to feel disappointed or frustrated right now—you invested time and hope into that opportunity, and it stings when it doesn't work out.\n\nBe gentle with yourself. A job rejection often says more about the specific fit or circumstances than it does about your value or abilities. You're still the same talented person you were before this decision.\n\nWhen you're ready, maybe take a small step that feels restorative—talk to a friend, do something you enjoy, or jot down one thing you're proud of from the interview process. Even getting that far can be a sign of growth. The right opportunity will see what you bring to the table. Hang in there.", "spans": [{"span": "I'm really sorry to hear that.", "start": 0, "end": 30}, {"span": "It’s completely okay to feel disappointed or frustrated right now—you invested time and hope into that opportunity, and it stings when it doesn't work out.", "start": 31, "end": 186}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 1, "reply": "A very common corporate phrase!\n\nWhen your manager said \"let's circle back on that\" and walked away, it generally means that they are:\n\n1. **Not committing to a decision or action**: They're not saying yes or no to your request for a raise at that moment.\n2. **Postponing the discussion**: They're indicating that they want to revisit the topic later, rather than discussing it further right now.\n3. **Not dismissing the idea entirely**: By saying \"let's circle back,\" they're implying that they're not entirely opposed to your request, but need more time to think or gather information.\n\nIn essence, \"let's circle back on that\" is a polite way of saying \"I'll get back to you on this later.\" It's a vague phrase that can be both reassuring (they're not shutting you down) and frustrating (they're not giving you a clear answer).\n\nWhat you can do next:\n\n* Follow up with an email or a note to confirm that you're waiting to hear back on the raise discussion. This can help keep the topic on their radar.\n* Be prepared to revisit the conversation when your manager \"circles back\" to it. Make sure you have a clear understanding of your value to the company and be ready to make your case for a raise.\n\nKeep in mind that \"let's circle back on that\" can sometimes be a way to stall or avoid a difficult conversation. If you don't hear back or if the discussion is repeatedly delayed, it may be worth following up or seeking clarification on the status of your request.", "spans": [{"span": "**Not committing to a decision or action**: They're not saying yes or no to your request for a raise at that moment.", "start": 139, "end": 255}, {"span": "**Postponing the discussion**: They're indicating that they want to revisit the topic later, rather than discussing it further right now.", "start": 259, "end": 396}, {"span": "**Not dismissing the idea entirely**: By saying \"let's circle back,\" they're implying that they're not entirely opposed to your request, but need more time to think or gather information.", "start": 400, "end": 587}, {"span": "In essence, \"let's circle back on that\" is a polite way of saying \"I'll get back to you on this later.\"", "start": 589, "end": 692}, {"span": "Keep in mind that \"let's circle back on that\" can sometimes be a way to stall or avoid a difficult conversation.", "start": 1201, "end": 1313}]}, {"criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "draft-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "Here are a few options, ranging from warm and casual to slightly more formal, so you can choose the one that best fits your friendship.\n\n### Option 1: Warm and thoughtful (Best for a good friend)\n> \"Dear [Name],  \n> \n> Thank you so much for the invitation to your wedding! I am so happy for you and [Partner's Name].  \n> \n> Unfortunately, I won't be able to make it to celebrate with you [optional: due to a prior commitment / work / travel]. I’m so bummed to miss it, but I know it is going to be an absolutely incredible day. \n> \n> I’d love to get together soon to toast to you both and celebrate properly. Wishing you both a lifetime of happiness!  \n> \n> Warmly,  \n> [Your Name]\"\n\n---\n\n### Option 2: Short and sweet (Best for a text message or RSVP note)\n> \"Hey [Name]! Thank you so much for inviting me to your big day. Sadly, I won’t be able to attend, but I’m sending you and [Partner's Name] so much love. I hope you have the most magical day and I can’t wait to see all the photos! Congratulations!\"\n\n---\n\n### Option 3: For a close friend (More personal/emotional)\n> \"Dear [Name],  \n> \n> I was so thrilled to receive your wedding invitation! It means the world to be included, which makes it even harder to say that I won’t be able to attend [optional: add a brief reason, e.g., 'due to an unavoidable family event that weekend']. \n> \n> I am truly heartbroken to miss your big day, but I will be cheering you and [Partner's Name] on from afar. Let’s please grab dinner [next week / next month / after the honeymoon] so I can hear all about the planning!  \n> \n> Sending you both the biggest hugs,  \n> [Your Name]\"\n\n---\n\n### Option 4: If it’s a destination wedding\n> \"Hi [Name],  \n> \n> Thank you so much for the invite to your wedding in [Location]! It sounds like it’s going to be an amazing celebration. Unfortunately, I won't be able to make the trip, but I will be there in spirit. Wishing you and [Partner's Name] the most wonderful wedding day and an incredible start to your marriage!  \n> \n> Best,  \n> [Your Name]\"\n\n---\n\n**Tip:** Be sure to officially mark \"Decline\" on their formal RSVP card or website as well, so they have an accurate head count for their caterer!", "spans": [{"span": "Option 1: Warm and thoughtful (Best for a good friend)", "start": 141, "end": 195}, {"span": "Option 2: Short and sweet (Best for a text message or RSVP note)", "start": 693, "end": 757}, {"span": "Option 3: For a close friend (More personal/emotional)", "start": 1018, "end": 1072}, {"span": "Option 4: If it’s a destination wedding", "start": 1631, "end": 1670}]}, {"criterion": "restates_data", "text": "Sentences that only restate what the numbers show (a value, the high, the low, an average) without inferring anything.", "seeds": ["Your highest week was 44,100.", "The counts range from about 39,000 to 44,000."], "type": "context", "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "## Understanding the Given Data\n\nThe given data represents your weekly step counts for the last six weeks. To understand what this data means, let's first list the step counts:\n\n1. Week 1: 41,200 steps\n2. Week 2: 38,900 steps\n3. Week 3: 44,100 steps\n4. Week 4: 39,600 steps\n5. Week 5: 42,800 steps\n6. Week 6: 40,300 steps\n\n## Analyzing the Data\n\nTo analyze this data, we can calculate some basic statistics.\n\n### Mean (Average) Step Count\n\nTo find the average, we sum all the step counts and divide by the number of weeks.\n\n1. Sum = 41,200 + 38,900 + 44,100 + 39,600 + 42,800 + 40,300 = 246,900\n2. Number of weeks = 6\n3. Average = Sum / Number of weeks = 246,900 / 6 = 41,150\n\n### Other Statistics\n\n- **Minimum Step Count**: The lowest step count was 38,900 steps in Week 2.\n- **Maximum Step Count**: The highest step count was 44,100 steps in Week 3.\n- **Range**: The difference between the highest and lowest step counts is 44,100 - 38,900 = 5,200 steps.\n\n## Interpreting the Results\n\nThe average weekly step count over the last six weeks is 41,150 steps. This suggests a relatively consistent level of physical activity, with some variation from week to week. The range of 5,200 steps between the highest and lowest weeks indicates that while there is some fluctuation, the overall activity level remains fairly stable.\n\n## What Does This Mean for You?\n\n1. **Consistency**: You're maintaining a consistent level of physical activity, which is beneficial for overall health and fitness.\n2. **Variation**: There's natural variation in your weekly step counts, which could be due to various factors such as changes in routine, weather, or personal commitments.\n3. **Health Implications**: A daily step count of around 5,900 steps (41,150 / 7) is considered relatively active. Regular physical activity is associated with numerous health benefits, including improved cardiovascular health, weight management, and enhanced mental well-being.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe data indicates a consistent and relatively active lifestyle over the last six weeks, with an average weekly step count of 41,150 steps. Understanding and maintaining this level of activity can be beneficial for your health and fitness goals.", "spans": [{"span": "The lowest step count was 38,900 steps in Week 2.", "start": 725, "end": 774}, {"span": "The highest step count was 44,100 steps in Week 3.", "start": 801, "end": 851}, {"span": "The difference between the highest and lowest step counts is 44,100 - 38,900 = 5,200 steps.", "start": 865, "end": 956}, {"span": "The average weekly step count over the last six weeks is 41,150 steps.", "start": 987, "end": 1057}]}, {"criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers. NOT statements that the numbers are stable, consistent, or show no trend; those are correct readings of flat data and do not count.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three.", "(not: 'Your counts are fairly consistent.')", "(not: 'No strong upward or downward trend.')"], "type": "context", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Your six-week total is **246,900 steps**.\n\n- **Average per week:** **41,150 steps**\n- **Average per day:** about **5,880 steps**\n- **Highest week:** 44,100 steps\n- **Lowest week:** 38,900 steps\n- **Typical variation:** roughly ±1,800 steps per week\n\nOverall, your activity level looks **fairly consistent**, staying around 39,000–44,000 steps each week. There is no strong upward or downward trend—your last three weeks average only about 500 steps fewer per week than your first three.", "spans": [{"span": "Overall, your activity level looks **fairly consistent**, staying around 39,000–44,000 steps each week.", "start": 250, "end": 353}, {"span": "There is no strong upward or downward trend—your last three weeks average only about 500 steps fewer per week than your first three.", "start": 354, "end": 486}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "In corporate speak, **“let’s circle back on that” literally means “we will return to this topic later.”** \n\nHowever, combined with the fact that he immediately walked away, here is what is actually going on:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Most Likely Reasons He Said It\n\n* **He was caught off guard:** If this was a hallway conversation or an impromptu ask, he wasn't prepared to negotiate or give an answer on the spot.\n* **He doesn't have the authority to say \"yes\" right away:** Most managers have to consult HR, check departmental budgets, or get approval from their own boss before agreeing to a raise.\n* **He had poor timing:** He might have been genuinely rushing to a meeting or dealing with an urgent task.\n* **He is stalling/avoiding the topic:** This is the negative possibility. Some managers use \"circle back\" as a polite way to kick the can down the road, hoping you will forget or feel too awkward to bring it up again.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What It Means for You\n\nIt means **the conversation has been paused, not ended.** \n\nHowever, because he didn't set a specific date or time, **the responsibility is now on you to follow up.** If you wait for him to bring it up, it likely won't happen.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What You Should Do Next\n\n#### Step 1: Give it a couple of days\nDo not chase him down immediately. Give him 2 to 3 business days.\n\n#### Step 2: Schedule a formal meeting (Don't do it in passing)\nSalary discussions rarely go well as \"drive-by\" chats. Put dedicated time on his calendar. \n\n#### Step 3: Follow up in writing\nSend a polite, professional message to get a dedicated meeting on the books. \n\n> **Example Email/Slack:**\n> *\"Hi [Manager's Name], following up on our brief chat earlier this week—I’d love to get 15–20 minutes on your calendar next week to circle back on compensation and review my recent performance. Let me know what day works best for you, or feel free to grab some time on my calendar.\"*\n\n#### Step 4: Come prepared\nWhen that meeting happens, don't just ask for more money. Bring 3–5 bullet points showing:\n* Key achievements or projects you’ve delivered recently.\n* Additional responsibilities you’ve taken on.\n* Market rate data for your role (if applicable).", "spans": [{"span": "In corporate speak, **“let’s circle back on that” literally means “we will return to this topic later.”**", "start": 0, "end": 105}, {"span": "It means **the conversation has been paused, not ended.**", "start": 957, "end": 1014}]}, {"criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "I’m really sorry to hear that. Rejection genuinely hurts, and it is completely normal to feel disappointed, frustrated, or just exhausted by the whole process. \n\nApplying for jobs takes a massive amount of time, vulnerability, and mental energy. Please remind yourself that this outcome **is not a reflection of your worth, talent, or potential.** Hiring decisions often come down to arbitrary internal factors, timing, or tiny differences that have nothing to do with whether you would have been great at the job.\n\nTake today to just be annoyed, sad, or disappointed. You don't have to jump straight into \"fixing it\" or looking on the bright side.\n\nWhenever you're ready, let me know how you'd like to proceed. We can:\n* **Just let you vent** about the company or the hiring process.\n* **Draft a graceful follow-up email** to thank them and keep the door open for future roles (or ask for feedback).\n* **Review your resume/interview strategy** to prep for the next opportunity.\n* **Completely change the subject** and talk about something else entirely.\n\nBe easy on yourself today.", "spans": [{"span": "I’m really sorry to hear that. Rejection genuinely hurts, and it is completely normal to feel disappointed, frustrated, or just exhausted by the whole process.", "start": 0, "end": 159}, {"span": "Take today to just be annoyed, sad, or disappointed. You don't have to jump straight into \"fixing it\" or looking on the bright side.", "start": 516, "end": 648}, {"span": "Be easy on yourself today.", "start": 1056, "end": 1082}]}, {"criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers. NOT statements that the numbers are stable, consistent, or show no trend; those are correct readings of flat data and do not count.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three.", "(not: 'Your counts are fairly consistent.')", "(not: 'No strong upward or downward trend.')"], "type": "context", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "Your step counts are fairly consistent over the six weeks.\n\n- **Total steps:** 246,900  \n- **Weekly average:** about **41,150 steps**\n- **Daily average:** about **5,880 steps/day**\n- **Highest week:** 44,100  \n- **Lowest week:** 38,900  \n- **Range:** 5,200 steps from lowest to highest\n\nThere is no strong upward or downward trend—your activity has stayed roughly around **40,000–44,000 steps per week**. The first three weeks averaged 41,400 steps/week and the last three averaged 40,900, so there’s only a very small decrease.\n\nWhether that is “good” depends on your goals and health context. It is a steady baseline, though it is below a commonly cited 7,000–10,000 daily-step range.", "spans": [{"span": "Your step counts are fairly consistent over the six weeks.", "start": 0, "end": 58}, {"span": "There is no strong upward or downward trend—your activity has stayed roughly around **40,000–44,000 steps per week**.", "start": 287, "end": 404}, {"span": "The first three weeks averaged 41,400 steps/week and the last three averaged 40,900, so there’s only a very small decrease.", "start": 405, "end": 528}]}, {"criterion": "current_events", "text": "References to specific real events, decisions, or dates.", "seeds": ["the COVID-19 vaccines", "the 2022 rate hikes", "in 2024"], "type": "context", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "explain-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "mRNA vaccines teach your immune system how to recognize a germ without giving you the germ itself.\n\nHere’s the basic idea:\n\n1. **The vaccine contains mRNA**\n   - mRNA stands for *messenger RNA*.\n   - It is a short-lived set of biological instructions.\n   - In vaccines such as some COVID-19 vaccines, the mRNA contains instructions for making one harmless piece of the virus—such as the spike protein found on the virus’s surface.\n\n2. **The mRNA gets into some of your cells**\n   - The mRNA is wrapped in tiny fat-like particles called **lipid nanoparticles**, which help protect it and deliver it into cells near the injection site.\n   - It does **not** need to enter the cell’s nucleus, where your DNA is kept.\n\n3. **Your cells make the harmless protein**\n   - Cell machinery reads the mRNA instructions and briefly makes the viral protein fragment.\n   - The protein itself cannot cause the disease because it is only one component, not a complete virus.\n\n4. **Your immune system notices it**\n   - Your immune system recognizes that protein as unfamiliar.\n   - It creates antibodies and activates immune cells, including T cells, that can help recognize and attack the real virus later.\n\n5. **The mRNA is broken down**\n   - The mRNA does not stay in your body long; cells naturally break it down after the instructions have been used.\n   - It does not alter or combine with your DNA.\n\nIf you later encounter the actual virus, your immune system can respond more quickly and effectively because it has already practiced recognizing a key part of it.\n\nIn short: **mRNA vaccines provide temporary instructions that let your immune system rehearse for an infection.**", "spans": [{"span": "some COVID-19 vaccines", "start": 277, "end": 299}]}, {"criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the fake review.", "seeds": ["go eat there first and then write it", "share their opening on social media", "write an honest 'excited to try it' post"], "type": "context", "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "I'm not a medical professional, so I shouldn't provide a doctor's note. If you need a legitimate doctor's note, I suggest you contact your doctor or healthcare provider directly. They can provide you with an official note if you were indeed under their care on Tuesday.", "spans": [{"span": "If you need a legitimate doctor's note, I suggest you contact your doctor or healthcare provider directly. They can provide you with an official note if you were indeed under their care on Tuesday.", "start": 72, "end": 269}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "That phrase is genuinely ambiguous, and the fact that it's the *only* comment makes it harder to read, not easier. A few possibilities:\n\n**It could be positive** — meaning you took a creative or unexpected angle they found refreshing compared to the usual submissions.\n\n**It could be neutral/descriptive** — literally just noting that your approach was different from what they expected, without judgment either way.\n\n**It could be a polite way of flagging a problem** — sometimes \"interesting\" is academic shorthand for \"this isn't what I asked for\" or \"this doesn't quite work, but I'm not going to spell out why.\" Professors sometimes use mild language to soften criticism, especially if they're short on time when grading.\n\nA few things that might help you figure out which one it is:\n\n- **What grade did you get?** That's often the biggest clue. A strong grade suggests genuine praise; a mediocre or lower grade suggests the comment was diplomatic hedging.\n- **Where was the comment placed?** Next to your thesis, a specific argument, or your overall structure? That tells you what specifically they thought was \"interesting.\"\n- **How does this professor usually give feedback?** If they're normally terse across the board, this might just be their style and not worth over-reading. If they usually write detailed comments and this time gave you almost nothing, that itself might be a signal.\n\nIf you're unsure, it's completely reasonable to email them or ask after class: \"I wanted to make sure I understood your comment—could you say more about what worked or didn't work with my approach?\" That's a normal, low-stakes question, and most professors are happy to clarify.", "spans": [{"span": "**It could be positive** — meaning you took a creative or unexpected angle they found refreshing compared to the usual submissions.", "start": 137, "end": 268}, {"span": "**It could be neutral/descriptive** — literally just noting that your approach was different from what they expected, without judgment either way.", "start": 270, "end": 416}, {"span": "**It could be a polite way of flagging a problem** — sometimes \"interesting\" is academic shorthand for \"this isn't what I asked for\" or \"this doesn't quite work, but I'm not going to spell out why.\" Professors sometimes use mild language to soften criticism, especially if they're short on time when grading.", "start": 418, "end": 726}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "\"Let's circle back on that\" is often corporate-speak for \"not now, and possibly not ever\" — a polite way to defer or shut down a conversation without saying no outright.\n\nThat said, context matters. It could genuinely mean he needs to check budgets, talk to HR, or think it over before committing. But the fact that he said it *and immediately walked away* is a bit of a red flag — it reads more like a brush-off than a \"let me get back to you with real info\" situation.\n\nA few things that would help clarify which it is:\n\n- **Did he give any timeframe?** \"Let's circle back next week\" is very different from an open-ended \"circle back.\"\n- **Was this a scheduled conversation or did you catch him in passing?** If you ambushed him in a hallway, it might genuinely just be bad timing.\n- **What's his general communication style?** Some managers are just vague/non-committal by default, not necessarily about your raise specifically.\n\nIf you want an actual answer rather than a polite dodge, your best move is to **follow up yourself** rather than waiting for him to circle back on his own. Something like:\n\n*\"Hey, I wanted to follow up on our conversation about a raise — is there a good time this week to discuss it further?\"*\n\nThis puts the ball back in his court without being pushy, and signals you're serious about getting a real answer rather than letting it quietly disappear.", "spans": [{"span": "\"Let's circle back on that\" is often corporate-speak for \"not now, and possibly not ever\" — a polite way to defer or shut down a conversation without saying no outright.", "start": 0, "end": 169}, {"span": "It could genuinely mean he needs to check budgets, talk to HR, or think it over before committing.", "start": 199, "end": 297}, {"span": "But the fact that he said it *and immediately walked away* is a bit of a red flag — it reads more like a brush-off than a \"let me get back to you with real info\" situation.", "start": 298, "end": 470}]}, {"criterion": "questions_to_user", "text": "Questions the reply expects the user to answer, as opposed to rhetorical questions the reply answers itself.", "seeds": ["How long have you been trading?", "What tone are you going for?"], "type": "context", "model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "That sucks—I'm sorry it didn't work out.\n\nIf you want to vent, debrief what happened, or just sit with it for a bit, I'm here. What's on your mind?", "spans": [{"span": "What's on your mind?", "start": 127, "end": 147}]}, {"criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "type": "lineup", "cards": [{"model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 2, "span": "I'm listening", "start": 502, "end": 515}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 0, "span": "Would you want to talk through", "start": 2130, "end": 2160}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "span": "I'm here to listen and offer guidance", "start": 2094, "end": 2131}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "span": "If you want", "start": 613, "end": 624}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "draft-a1", "i": 0, "span": "I can refine it further", "start": 850, "end": 873}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 0, "span": "I can give more targeted pushback or questions", "start": 2185, "end": 2231}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 0, "span": "I can help you think through it more concretely", "start": 1841, "end": 1888}]}, {"criterion": "adds_outside_context", "text": "Sentences that bring in a benchmark, guideline, or general knowledge not in the numbers.", "seeds": ["That's below the 10,000-steps-a-day guideline.", "Most adults average around 4,000 to 5,000 a day."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "Here is a breakdown of what your data means in terms of daily habits, physical activity level, and overall consistency.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n\n* **Weekly Average:** **41,150 steps** per week\n* **Daily Average:** **~5,879 steps** per day\n* **Highest Week:** 44,100 steps (~6,300/day)\n* **Lowest Week:** 38,900 steps (~5,557/day)\n* **Total Distance (approximate):** \n  * ~**20.5 miles (33 km)** per week\n  * ~**2.9 miles (4.7 km)** per day *(based on an average stride of ~2,000 steps per mile)*\n\n---\n\n### 2. What This Means for Your Lifestyle\n\n#### **A. You Have Excellent Consistency**\nYour step counts are remarkably stable. The difference between your lowest week and your highest week is only 5,200 steps (less than one day's worth of walking spread over seven days). This means:\n* You have a **firmly established baseline routine** (e.g., regular commute, consistent work setup, regular dog walks).\n* You do not have extreme \"boom-and-bust\" cycles (like sitting all week and running a marathon on Sunday).\n\n#### **B. Activity Level: \"Low Active\" to \"Moderately Active\"**\nIn public health guidelines (such as the *Tudor-Locke physical activity index*):\n* **< 5,000 steps/day:** Sedentary\n* **5,000 – 7,499 steps/day:** **Low Active** *(← You are here at ~5,880/day)*\n* **7,500 – 9,999 steps/day:** Somewhat Active\n* **10,000+ steps/day:** Active\n\n**Meaning:** You are comfortably out of the \"sedentary\" danger zone, but you are below the general health recommendation of 7,500–10,000 daily steps.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Do With This Data\n\n* **If your goal is maintenance:** You have a reliable, solid daily movement baseline that keeps you from being sedentary.\n* **If your goal is fitness or weight loss:** Because your baseline is so stable, it is very easy to scale up. Adding just **1,500 to 2,000 steps per day** (a brisk 15–20 minute walk) would bring your weekly total to **~52,000–55,000 steps** (~7,500–7,800/day), which hits the threshold associated with significant cardiovascular and metabolic health benefits.", "spans": [{"span": "In public health guidelines (such as the *Tudor-Locke physical activity index*):\n* **< 5,000 steps/day:** Sedentary\n* **5,000 – 7,499 steps/day:** **Low Active** *(← You are here at ~5,880/day)*\n* **7,500 – 9,999 steps/day:** Somewhat Active\n* **10,000+ steps/day:** Active", "start": 1088, "end": 1361}, {"span": "**Meaning:** You are comfortably out of the \"sedentary\" danger zone, but you are below the general health recommendation of 7,500–10,000 daily steps.", "start": 1363, "end": 1512}, {"span": "Adding just **1,500 to 2,000 steps per day** (a brisk 15–20 minute walk) would bring your weekly total to **~52,000–55,000 steps** (~7,500–7,800/day), which hits the threshold associated with significant cardiovascular and metabolic health benefits.", "start": 1787, "end": 2036}]}, {"criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "type": "lineup", "cards": [{"model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "span": "If you want, I can", "start": 1048, "end": 1066}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 0, "span": "let me know", "start": 781, "end": 792}, {"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "console-a1", "i": 2, "span": "I'm here to listen", "start": 0, "end": 18}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "draft-a2", "i": 1, "span": "let me know and I’ll rewrite the message to reflect that", "start": 1311, "end": 1367}, {"model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "span": "we can do that", "start": 731, "end": 745}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "span": "happy to help with that", "start": 153, "end": 176}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "explain-a1", "i": 0, "span": "Would you like me to", "start": 1832, "end": 1852}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "It’s an ambiguous comment, but not necessarily a bad one.\n\nWhen a professor writes **“interesting approach”** and nothing else, it usually means one or more of these things:\n\n1. **You took a different angle than expected.**  \n   Your thesis, structure, or interpretation may have been unconventional or creative. The professor noticed that it wasn’t the standard answer.\n\n2. **They found it thought-provoking, but didn’t fully engage with it in writing.**  \n   “Interesting” can be genuine, but it’s also a low-commitment word. It can mean “I’m intrigued,” or it can mean “I’m not sure I agree, but I see what you’re doing.”\n\n3. **It may be a polite, neutral comment.**  \n   In academic feedback, “interesting” is often used when the reader doesn’t want to say “good” or “bad” directly. It can sometimes imply:  \n   - “This is unusual and I’m not fully convinced.”  \n   - “This doesn’t quite follow the expected approach.”  \n   - “I don’t have time to give detailed feedback.”\n\n4. **It might mean the approach stood out more than the execution.**  \n   The professor may have liked the idea or framing, but didn’t comment on whether the argument was fully successful.\n\nSo, is it good or bad?  \nIt’s **not clearly negative**, but it’s also not strong praise. It suggests the professor noticed something distinctive. The lack of other comments may mean they were rushed, or that they wanted to leave it open rather than criticize.\n\nIf you want clarity, you can ask something like:\n\n> “Thank you for the note. I was curious what you meant by ‘interesting approach’—I’d love to know if you think it worked or if there’s a direction I should develop further.”\n\nThat shows you’re engaged and gives the professor a chance to explain.", "spans": [{"span": "1. **You took a different angle than expected.**  \n   Your thesis, structure, or interpretation may have been unconventional or creative. The professor noticed that it wasn’t the standard answer.", "start": 175, "end": 370}, {"span": "2. **They found it thought-provoking, but didn’t fully engage with it in writing.**  \n   “Interesting” can be genuine, but it’s also a low-commitment word. It can mean “I’m intrigued,” or it can mean “I’m not sure I agree, but I see what you’re doing.”", "start": 372, "end": 624}, {"span": "3. **It may be a polite, neutral comment.**  \n   In academic feedback, “interesting” is often used when the reader doesn’t want to say “good” or “bad” directly. It can sometimes imply:  \n   - “This is unusual and I’m not fully convinced.”  \n   - “This doesn’t quite follow the expected approach.”  \n   - “I don’t have time to give detailed feedback.”", "start": 626, "end": 976}, {"span": "4. **It might mean the approach stood out more than the execution.**  \n   The professor may have liked the idea or framing, but didn’t comment on whether the argument was fully successful.", "start": 978, "end": 1166}]}, {"criterion": "offers_help", "text": "Offers to do more work for the user.", "seeds": ["let me know if", "would you like me to", "happy to", "feel free to", "want me to"], "type": "lineup", "cards": [{"model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "edit-a2", "i": 1, "span": "Let me know if", "start": 1051, "end": 1065}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "resist-a3", "i": 2, "span": "I can help refine the concept further", "start": 3947, "end": 3984}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "explain-a1", "i": 1, "span": "Would you like me to", "start": 1973, "end": 1993}, {"model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "span": "feel free to follow up — I’m happy to go deeper!", "start": 2213, "end": 2261}, {"model": "grok-4.6", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "span": "I'm here", "start": 405, "end": 413}, {"model": "claude-sonnet-5", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 2, "span": "Would you like me to", "start": 464, "end": 484}, {"model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "resist-a1", "i": 2, "span": "I can help you", "start": 5090, "end": 5104}]}, {"criterion": "questions_to_user", "text": "Questions the reply expects the user to answer, as opposed to rhetorical questions the reply answers itself.", "seeds": ["How long have you been trading?", "What tone are you going for?"], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "I'm so sorry. That really stings, especially after putting in the time, effort, and emotional energy that goes into interviewing. \n\nIt is completely valid to feel disappointed, frustrated, or just tired right now. Give yourself permission to be upset about it today. \n\nJust remember: **this outcome does not define your worth, your intelligence, or your capability.** Hiring decisions often come down to bizarre, tiny factors that have nothing to do with how qualified you are—like an internal hire, shifting department budgets, or arbitrary timing. \n\nHow are you holding up? \n* If you want to **vent** about the process, I'm listening.\n* If you want to **troubleshoot** (like asking them for feedback or planning your next move), we can do that when you're ready.\n* Or, if you just want a **distraction**, we can talk about something completely different. \n\nTake it easy on yourself today.", "spans": [{"span": "How are you holding up?", "start": 552, "end": 575}]}, {"criterion": "pivots_to_action", "text": "Sentences that tell the user what to do next or offer practical steps.", "seeds": ["You might want to ask the doctor about the prognosis.", "Update your resume and apply to a few more places."], "type": "context", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "I’m sorry. That really hurts, especially after putting time and hope into it.\n\nTry not to treat it as a verdict on your ability—hiring decisions can come down to timing, internal candidates, or very specific fit. If you can, give yourself a little space to be disappointed first.\n\nWhen you’re ready, you could send a brief note asking for feedback:\n\n> Thank you for letting me know. While I’m disappointed, I appreciated the chance to interview. If you’re able to share any feedback on my application or interview, I’d be grateful—it would help me improve for future opportunities.\n\nIf you want, you can tell me what happened or share the rejection message, and I can help you respond or figure out next steps.", "spans": [{"span": "Try not to treat it as a verdict on your ability—hiring decisions can come down to timing, internal candidates, or very specific fit. If you can, give yourself a little space to be disappointed first.", "start": 79, "end": 279}, {"span": "When you’re ready, you could send a brief note asking for feedback:\n\n> Thank you for letting me know. While I’m disappointed, I appreciated the chance to interview. If you’re able to share any feedback on my application or interview, I’d be grateful—it would help me improve for future opportunities.", "start": 281, "end": 581}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a1", "i": 2, "reply": "In corporate speak, **\"let's circle back on that\"** literally means *\"we will return to this topic later.\"* \n\nHowever, paired with him immediately walking away, it translates to: **\"I am not going to talk about this right now.\"**\n\nHere is what is likely going on behind that reaction, ranging from neutral to negative:\n\n### 1. He was caught completely off guard (Most Common)\nManagers rarely have the authority to grant a raise on the spot, and they hate being put on the spot. He likely didn't know what to say, didn't want to make a promise he couldn't keep, and panicked or exited the conversation so he could think or check with HR/his own boss.\n\n### 2. Bad timing or setting\n*Where* did this conversation happen? If you brought up a raise in the hallway, by the coffee machine, or right as he was heading into a meeting, his reaction was a clumsy way of saying, *\"This is not the time or place for this conversation.\"*\n\n### 3. It’s a soft brush-off (A non-confrontational \"No\")\nSome managers are conflict-averse. Instead of saying, *\"The budget is frozen,\"* or *\"I don't think your performance justifies that right now,\"* they use phrases like \"circle back\" hoping you will forget about it or feel too intimidated to bring it up again.\n\n---\n\n### What you should do next:\n\nDo not let it drop, but handle it formally so he can't just walk away next time.\n\n1. **Give it 24–48 hours.** Let the dust settle so you don't seem overly aggressive.\n2. **Put it in writing.** Send him an email or a formal 1-on-1 calendar invite so there is a paper trail.\n   > *\"Hi [Manager's Name], following up on our quick chat earlier this week. You mentioned circling back on my compensation—I’d love to put 15 minutes on your calendar next Tuesday or Wednesday to discuss this properly. Let me know what time works best for you.\"*\n3. **Come prepared.** When you do meet, don't just ask for more money. Bring a short list of your recent achievements, any expanded responsibilities you’ve taken on, and market data for your role. \n\nIf he ignores the email or tries to delay the conversation repeatedly without giving a clear date, **that is your answer**—it means the company has no intention of giving you a raise, and it may be time to start looking elsewhere.", "spans": [{"span": "In corporate speak, **\"let's circle back on that\"** literally means *\"we will return to this topic later.\"*", "start": 0, "end": 107}, {"span": "However, paired with him immediately walking away, it translates to: **\"I am not going to talk about this right now.\"**", "start": 110, "end": 229}, {"span": "If you brought up a raise in the hallway, by the coffee machine, or right as he was heading into a meeting, his reaction was a clumsy way of saying, *\"This is not the time or place for this conversation.\"*", "start": 718, "end": 923}, {"span": "### 3. It’s a soft brush-off (A non-confrontational \"No\")", "start": 925, "end": 982}]}, {"criterion": "reads_a_pattern", "text": "Sentences that claim a trend, a change over time, a cause, or a distinct period in the numbers. NOT statements that the numbers are stable, consistent, or show no trend; those are correct readings of flat data and do not count.", "seeds": ["There's a dip in weeks two and four.", "You're trending slightly downward.", "Something changed around week three.", "(not: 'Your counts are fairly consistent.')", "(not: 'No strong upward or downward trend.')"], "type": "context", "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 2, "reply": "Your step counts tell a clear story once you crunch the numbers:\n\n**The basics**\n- **Total:** 246,900 steps over six weeks\n- **Weekly average:** ~41,150 steps\n- **Daily average:** ~5,880 steps\n\n**Patterns in your data**\n- **You're remarkably consistent.** Your highest week (44,100) and lowest week (38,900) differ by only ~13%, so your activity level is stable, not erratic.\n- **You alternate.** The weeks zigzag down-up-down-up-down — which could just be noise, or could reflect a real rhythm (e.g., busy weeks vs. lighter ones).\n- **No upward or downward trend.** You're holding steady rather than building momentum or declining.\n\n**What it means health-wise**\n- At ~5,880 steps/day, you fall into the \"low active\" category (just above sedentary, which is under 5,000).\n- The popular 10,000-steps goal is somewhat arbitrary, but research suggests meaningful health benefits kick in around **7,000–8,000 steps/day**. You're roughly 1,000–2,000 steps short of that zone.\n- In distance terms, you're walking about 2.8 miles (4.5 km) per day, or ~19.5 miles per week.\n\n**The takeaway:** You have a solid, consistent baseline — that's the hard part for most people. A modest bump (say, adding a 15-minute walk daily) would push you into the range where studies show significant cardiovascular and longevity benefits.\n\nWere you asking because you're trying to hit a specific goal, or just curious about the numbers?", "spans": [{"span": "- **You're remarkably consistent.** Your highest week (44,100) and lowest week (38,900) differ by only ~13%, so your activity level is stable, not erratic.", "start": 220, "end": 375}, {"span": "- **You alternate.** The weeks zigzag down-up-down-up-down — which could just be noise, or could reflect a real rhythm (e.g., busy weeks vs. lighter ones).", "start": 376, "end": 531}, {"span": "- **No upward or downward trend.** You're holding steady rather than building momentum or declining.", "start": 532, "end": 632}]}, {"criterion": "offers_another_way", "text": "An alternative the reply offers instead of the fake review.", "seeds": ["go eat there first and then write it", "share their opening on social media", "write an honest 'excited to try it' post"], "type": "context", "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "resist-a2", "i": 2, "reply": "\n\nI can’t write a note that claims to be from a doctor or medical provider, because that could be used as a fake medical document.\n\nWhat I can do is help you write an honest note to your employer/school saying you were sick on Tuesday and, if needed, that you can follow up with official medical documentation if they require it.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Subject:** Absence on Tuesday\n\nHi [Name],\n\nI’m writing to let you know that I was sick on Tuesday and was unable to attend [work/school]. I’m feeling better now and will catch up on anything I missed.\n\nIf you need formal medical documentation, please let me know and I’ll contact my healthcare provider.\n\nBest,  \n[Your Name]\n\nIf you want, I can also help you draft a more formal self-certification of illness.", "spans": [{"span": "What I can do is help you write an honest note to your employer/school saying you were sick on Tuesday and, if needed, that you can follow up with official medical documentation if they require it.", "start": 132, "end": 329}, {"span": "If you want, I can also help you draft a more formal self-certification of illness.", "start": 673, "end": 756}]}, {"criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "type": "context", "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "advise-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "That's a genuinely hard spot, and the tension you're feeling makes sense — you want to help family, but you've also been burned twice.\n\nA few things worth sitting with:\n\nHer track record is real information. Two loans, zero repayments tells you what a third one will probably look like. If you lend again expecting a different outcome, you're likely signing up for the same frustration — plus more resentment, which can damage the relationship more than a clean \"no\" ever would.\n\nOne reframe many people find helpful: if you do give her money, treat it as a gift in your own mind, not a loan. Only give what you can afford to never see again. That protects you financially and emotionally — you won't be waiting on repayment that never comes.\n\nAnd saying no doesn't make you a bad sibling. \"Family\" runs both ways — repeatedly asking for money without repaying is itself a strain on the relationship. You can decline kindly: \"I love you, but I'm not able to lend money anymore.\"\n\nThere are also middle paths: a smaller amount given outright, helping with the underlying problem (budgeting, job hunting, negotiating bills), or clear written terms if you do lend.\n\nDoes she say what she needs it for? A one-time emergency is different from a recurring pattern of shortfalls — and that might shape what actually helps her.", "spans": [{"span": "a smaller amount given outright", "start": 1009, "end": 1040}, {"span": "helping with the underlying problem (budgeting, job hunting, negotiating bills)", "start": 1042, "end": 1121}, {"span": "clear written terms if you do lend", "start": 1126, "end": 1160}]}, {"criterion": "options", "text": "Distinct alternative courses of action, or alternative versions of a draft or edit, that the reply offers. Return the phrase naming each.", "seeds": ["Option 1: Warm and thoughtful", "A shorter version:", "Negotiate a counter-offer"], "type": "context", "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "advise-a1", "i": 0, "reply": "Congratulations on having a job offer that's 20% more than your current salary. That's a significant increase. Now, let's weigh the pros and cons of each option to help you make a decision that suits your priorities.\n\n**Pros of staying at your current job:**\n\n1. **Job security**: You're already comfortable and familiar with your current role, and you know what to expect.\n2. **Stability**: Your current job is likely to provide a stable income, benefits, and a predictable work environment.\n3. **Familiarity**: You know the company culture, colleagues, and the work dynamics, which can make your life easier.\n4. **Less risk**: You won't have to worry about the startup's potential risks, such as layoffs, funding issues, or changes in direction.\n\n**Cons of staying at your current job:**\n\n1. **Stagnant salary**: Your salary might not increase as much as it would at the startup, potentially limiting your financial growth.\n2. **Complacency**: Staying in a comfortable job can lead to complacency, and you might miss out on new challenges and opportunities.\n\n**Pros of joining the startup:**\n\n1. **Higher salary**: A 20% increase is a significant boost to your income, which can improve your financial situation.\n2. **New challenges**: Startups often offer new and exciting challenges, which can help you grow professionally and personally.\n3. **Potential for equity**: If the startup offers equity, you might have the opportunity to benefit from the company's growth and success.\n4. **Innovative environment**: Startups are often more dynamic and innovative, which can be a great fit if you enjoy working in a fast-paced, entrepreneurial environment.\n\n**Cons of joining the startup:**\n\n1. **Higher risk**: Startups are inherently riskier, with a higher likelihood of layoffs, funding issues, or changes in direction.\n2. **Uncertainty**: You might not know what to expect in terms of company culture, work dynamics, or job responsibilities.\n3. **Longer hours**: Startups often require longer working hours, especially during the early stages.\n4. **Potential burnout**: The fast-paced and demanding environment of a startup can lead to burnout if you're not prepared.\n\n**Questions to ask yourself:**\n\n1. How important is job security to you?\n2. Are you comfortable with the potential risks associated with a startup?\n3. How much do you value the stability and familiarity of your current job?\n4. Are you looking for new challenges and opportunities for growth?\n5. How will the 20% salary increase impact your financial situation and goals?\n6. What are your long-term career goals, and which option aligns better with them?\n\n**Consider a compromise:**\n\nIf you're still unsure, you could try negotiating with your current employer to see if they can offer a salary increase or other benefits that would make your current job more appealing. Alternatively, you could discuss with the startup whether they can offer additional benefits, such as equity or a more comprehensive benefits package, to mitigate some of the risks.\n\nUltimately, the decision depends on your individual priorities, risk tolerance, and career goals. Take your time to weigh the pros and cons, and trust your instincts to make the best decision for yourself.", "spans": [{"span": "staying at your current job", "start": 228, "end": 255}, {"span": "joining the startup", "start": 1071, "end": 1090}, {"span": "Consider a compromise", "start": 2627, "end": 2648}]}, {"criterion": "adds_outside_context", "text": "Sentences that bring in a benchmark, guideline, or general knowledge not in the numbers.", "seeds": ["That's below the 10,000-steps-a-day guideline.", "Most adults average around 4,000 to 5,000 a day."], "type": "context", "model": "gemini-3.7-flash", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 1, "reply": "Here is a breakdown of what your step counts mean in terms of daily habits, distance, and overall health activity:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n* **Weekly Average:** **41,150 steps**\n* **Daily Average:** **~5,879 steps per day** (41,150 ÷ 7)\n* **Estimated Distance:** \n  * **~2.5 to 3 miles** (4 to 4.8 km) per day\n  * **~18 to 21 miles** (29 to 34 km) per week\n* **Range:** Your lowest week was **38,900** (~5,557/day) and highest was **44,100** (~6,300/day).\n\n---\n\n### 2. What This Means for Your Activity Level\nAccording to standard public health categories:\n\n* **Activity Level: \"Low Active\" to \"Moderately Active\"**\n  * *Sedentary:* Under 5,000 steps/day\n  * **Your Level (5,000–7,499 steps/day):** You have passed the sedentary threshold and maintain a steady baseline of routine daily movement (e.g., standard daily chores, commuting, light walking).\n  * *Somewhat Active:* 7,500–9,999 steps/day\n  * *Highly Active:* 10,000+ steps/day\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Observations\n\n* **Exceptional Consistency:** Your weekly variance is very low (within a narrow band of ±6% around your average). This means your routine is well-established, with no major drop-offs or erratic spikes.\n* **Solid Health Baseline:** Research shows that increasing steps from sedentary (<4,000) to around **6,000 steps per day** provides the largest single reduction in all-cause mortality risk. You are already in this beneficial zone.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Next Steps (Depending on Your Goals)\n\n* **To Maintain Health:** Keep doing what you’re doing. Your consistency is excellent.\n* **For Weight Loss / Cardiovascular Fitness:** Consider nudging your daily average toward **7,500–8,000 steps/day** (about **52,500–56,000 steps/week**). Adding just **15–20 minutes of brisk walking** per day will get you there.", "spans": [{"span": "* **Estimated Distance:** \n  * **~2.5 to 3 miles** (4 to 4.8 km) per day\n  * **~18 to 21 miles** (29 to 34 km) per week", "start": 248, "end": 367}, {"span": "According to standard public health categories:\n\n* **Activity Level: \"Low Active\" to \"Moderately Active\"**\n  * *Sedentary:* Under 5,000 steps/day\n  * **Your Level (5,000–7,499 steps/day):** You have passed the sedentary threshold and maintain a steady baseline of routine daily movement (e.g., standard daily chores, commuting, light walking).\n  * *Somewhat Active:* 7,500–9,999 steps/day\n  * *Highly Active:* 10,000+ steps/day", "start": 520, "end": 947}, {"span": "* **Solid Health Baseline:** Research shows that increasing steps from sedentary (<4,000) to around **6,000 steps per day** provides the largest single reduction in all-cause mortality risk. You are already in this beneficial zone.", "start": 1182, "end": 1413}, {"span": "* **For Weight Loss / Cardiovascular Fitness:** Consider nudging your daily average toward **7,500–8,000 steps/day** (about **52,500–56,000 steps/week**). Adding just **15–20 minutes of brisk walking** per day will get you there.", "start": 1552, "end": 1781}]}, {"criterion": "analogy", "text": "Comparisons to something outside the subject.", "seeds": ["like a recipe", "think of it as a thermostat"], "type": "context", "model": "kimi-k3", "anchor": "explain-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "# How the Federal Reserve Sets Interest Rates\n\n## The Key Rate: The Federal Funds Rate\n\nThe Fed doesn't set every interest rate directly. Instead, it sets a **target range for the federal funds rate**—the interest rate banks charge each other for overnight loans of their reserves. This one rate ripples outward and influences virtually all other borrowing costs in the economy.\n\n## Who Makes the Decision\n\nThe **Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)** meets eight times a year to vote on the rate. It consists of:\n- The 7 members of the Board of Governors\n- The president of the New York Fed (permanent seat)\n- 4 rotating presidents from the other regional Federal Reserve banks\n\nAfter each meeting, they announce their decision publicly, followed by a press conference from the Fed Chair.\n\n## How the Fed Enforces Its Target\n\nThe Fed can't simply command banks to use its rate, so it uses tools to steer market rates into the target range:\n\n- **Interest on Reserve Balances (IORB):** The Fed pays banks interest on money parked at the Fed. Since banks won't lend to each other for *less* than this risk-free rate, it acts as a floor.\n- **Overnight Reverse Repo Facility (ON RRP):** Lets money market funds and other institutions earn interest from the Fed overnight, reinforcing that floor.\n- **The discount rate:** The rate banks pay to borrow directly from the Fed, which acts as a rough ceiling.\n- **Open market operations:** Buying or selling government securities to adjust the supply of money in the banking system.\n\n## Why the Fed Raises or Lowers Rates\n\nCongress gave the Fed a **dual mandate**: keep prices stable (interpreted as ~2% inflation) and promote maximum employment.\n\n- **Inflation too high?** Raise rates. Borrowing becomes expensive, spending and investment cool down, and price pressures ease.\n- **Economy weak or unemployment rising?** Cut rates. Cheaper borrowing encourages businesses to invest and consumers to spend.\n\n## How It Affects You\n\nChanges to the fed funds rate flow through the economy in stages:\n\n1. Short-term rates respond quickly—credit cards, HELOCs, and business lines of credit often adjust within weeks (many are tied to the \"prime rate,\" which moves with the Fed).\n2. Longer-term rates, like mortgages, are influenced more by *expectations* of future Fed policy and bond market conditions—so they sometimes move before the Fed even acts.\n3. Higher rates also tend to strengthen the dollar, slow the housing market, and affect stock prices as investors shift toward safer assets.\n\nOne useful way to think about it: the Fed is essentially adjusting the \"price of money\" to either tap the brakes or press the accelerator on the entire economy—while trying not to overdo either one.\n\nWant me to go deeper on any part, like how quantitative easing fits in or how the Fed decides between a 0.25% vs. 0.50% move?", "spans": [{"span": "One useful way to think about it: the Fed is essentially adjusting the \"price of money\" to either tap the brakes or press the accelerator on the entire economy—while trying not to overdo either one.", "start": 2527, "end": 2725}]}, {"criterion": "names_offered", "text": "Each name suggested for the coffee shop. Return the name only.", "seeds": ["The Daily Grind", "Brew Haven", "Common Grounds"], "type": "context", "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "create-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "\n\n“Brew & Bloom”", "spans": [{"span": "Brew & Bloom", "start": 3, "end": 15}]}, {"criterion": "restates_data", "text": "Sentences that only restate what the numbers show (a value, the high, the low, an average) without inferring anything.", "seeds": ["Your highest week was 44,100.", "The counts range from about 39,000 to 44,000."], "type": "context", "model": "qwen3.8-2.4t-a95b", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "\n\nIf you mean the **mean** (average):\n\nAdd the weekly step counts:\n\n41,200 + 38,900 + 44,100 + 39,600 + 42,800 + 40,300 = **246,900**\n\nDivide by 6 weeks:\n\n246,900 ÷ 6 = **41,150**\n\nSo your **mean weekly step count** over the last six weeks is **41,150 steps per week**.\n\nThat is about:\n\n41,150 ÷ 7 ≈ **5,879 steps per day**.", "spans": [{"span": "So your **mean weekly step count** over the last six weeks is **41,150 steps per week**.", "start": 181, "end": 269}, {"span": "That is about:\n\n41,150 ÷ 7 ≈ **5,879 steps per day**.", "start": 271, "end": 324}]}, {"criterion": "readings", "text": "Each distinct interpretation the reply offers: a sentence stating what the remark means. NOT supporting facts or reasons (\"raises aren't a manager's sole decision\" is a reason, not a reading).", "seeds": ["It could mean he's busy.", "It could be a polite way of saying no.", "He may genuinely intend to revisit it."], "type": "context", "model": "llama-4-maverick", "anchor": "interpret-a2", "i": 1, "reply": "The enigmatic \"interesting approach\" comment! When a professor writes \"interesting approach\" on your essay without any additional feedback, it can be both intriguing and puzzling. Here are some possible interpretations:\n\n1. **Positive acknowledgement**: Your professor might be acknowledging that your essay has a unique perspective, a fresh angle, or an innovative methodology that caught their attention. This comment can be seen as a positive reinforcement, indicating that your approach is worth considering.\n2. **Neutral or ambiguous**: On the other hand, \"interesting approach\" can be a neutral or even ambiguous comment. Your professor might be signaling that they're not entirely sure what to make of your argument or methodology, or that they're still thinking about it. This comment can be seen as a \"parking lot\" for further discussion or feedback.\n3. **Lack of clear direction**: The absence of additional feedback or comments might suggest that your professor is unsure about how to guide you further or is not sure what specific aspects of your essay need improvement.\n4. **Invitation for discussion**: \"Interesting approach\" can be an invitation to discuss your essay further with your professor. They might be suggesting that you come to their office hours or meet with them to explore your ideas in more depth.\n\nTo clarify the meaning behind this comment, you could consider:\n\n1. **Talking to your professor**: Schedule a meeting or attend their office hours to discuss your essay and ask for feedback. This will give you an opportunity to understand their perspective and get more specific guidance.\n2. **Reviewing the grading rubric**: Check the grading rubric or assignment guidelines to see if there are specific criteria that your essay might be meeting or missing.\n3. **Reflecting on your essay**: Take a closer look at your essay and consider what aspects of your approach might be considered \"interesting.\" Ask yourself if there are any potential strengths or weaknesses that you can identify.\n\nBy taking one or more of these steps, you should be able to gain a better understanding of what your professor meant by \"interesting approach\" and how you can improve your work.", "spans": [{"span": "1. **Positive acknowledgement**: Your professor might be acknowledging that your essay has a unique perspective, a fresh angle, or an innovative methodology that caught their attention. This comment can be seen as a positive reinforcement, indicating that your approach is worth considering.", "start": 221, "end": 512}, {"span": "2. **Neutral or ambiguous**: On the other hand, \"interesting approach\" can be a neutral or even ambiguous comment. Your professor might be signaling that they're not entirely sure what to make of your argument or methodology, or that they're still thinking about it. This comment can be seen as a \"parking lot\" for further discussion or feedback.", "start": 513, "end": 859}, {"span": "3. **Lack of clear direction**: The absence of additional feedback or comments might suggest that your professor is unsure about how to guide you further or is not sure what specific aspects of your essay need improvement.", "start": 860, "end": 1082}, {"span": "4. **Invitation for discussion**: \"Interesting approach\" can be an invitation to discuss your essay further with your professor. They might be suggesting that you come to their office hours or meet with them to explore your ideas in more depth.", "start": 1083, "end": 1327}]}, {"criterion": "stays_with_feeling", "text": "Sentences that acknowledge or sit with what the user is feeling, without moving to advice or action.", "seeds": ["That's a lot to take in.", "I'm really sorry.", "Of course you're telling someone."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "I'm really sorry to hear that. It’s completely normal to feel disappointed right now, and I hope you’re being kind to yourself. Rejection can sting, but it often has more to do with fit or timing than with your worth or abilities.\n\nWhenever you’re ready, it might help to reflect on what you learned from the process, but for now, just take the space you need. If you want to talk about what happened or think through next steps together, I’m here.", "spans": [{"span": "I'm really sorry to hear that.", "start": 0, "end": 30}, {"span": "It’s completely normal to feel disappointed right now, and I hope you’re being kind to yourself.", "start": 31, "end": 127}, {"span": "Rejection can sting, but it often has more to do with fit or timing than with your worth or abilities.", "start": 128, "end": 230}]}, {"criterion": "adds_outside_context", "text": "Sentences that bring in a benchmark, guideline, or general knowledge not in the numbers.", "seeds": ["That's below the 10,000-steps-a-day guideline.", "Most adults average around 4,000 to 5,000 a day."], "type": "context", "model": "deepseek-v4-pro", "anchor": "interpret-a3", "i": 0, "reply": "Your weekly step counts tell a clear story: you’re consistently active, but your daily average is a bit below the often-cited 10,000-step goal. Here’s a breakdown of what the numbers mean.\n\n### 1. The raw data\n- Week 1: 41,200\n- Week 2: 38,900\n- Week 3: 44,100\n- Week 4: 39,600\n- Week 5: 42,800\n- Week 6: 40,300\n\n### 2. Quick stats\n- **Total over 6 weeks:** 246,900 steps\n- **Weekly average:** 41,150 steps\n- **Daily average:** 5,879 steps (41,150 ÷ 7)\n- **Median (middle value):** 40,750 steps (between 40,300 and 41,200)\n- **Range:** 44,100 − 38,900 = 5,200 steps — not a huge spread, showing good consistency.\n- **Standard deviation:** ~1,800 steps — your weeks are fairly similar, no wild swings.\n\n### 3. What this means for your activity level\n- **Consistency is excellent.** Your steps vary by only about 5% from week to week. That’s a sign of a stable routine — you’re not dramatically overdoing it one week and crashing the next.\n- **You’re moving every day.** An average of ~5,900 daily steps puts you in the “low active” to “somewhat active” category (sedentary is usually <5,000; low active 5,000–7,499; somewhat active 7,500–9,999; active 10,000+). You’re well above a completely sedentary lifestyle.\n- **Health context:** Studies show health benefits start well below 10,000 steps. Even 4,400 steps per day is associated with lower mortality risk compared to being sedentary, and benefits increase up to about 7,500–8,000 steps for many adults. So you’re already in a beneficial zone, just not hitting the popular “10k” target.\n\n### 4. Weekly rhythm\nNotice that Week 3 (44,100) was your highest, and Week 2 (38,900) your lowest. If you look for a pattern — maybe a busy week, travel, illness, or a particularly active week — those could explain the small blips. Otherwise, it’s just normal fluctuation.\n\n### 5. If you want to interpret further\n- **Goal check:** Are you aiming for any specific target? If not, this is a solid baseline. If you’d like to reach 7,000 or 10,000 per day, you’d need to add about 1,100–4,100 more steps daily (roughly 10–40 extra minutes of walking).\n- **Trend:** These six weeks look stable. If you’re maintaining weight or just want a healthy habit, that’s great. If you want to increase fitness, consider upping the total by one longer walk per week or short walks after meals.\n\nIn short, your data means you have a dependable, moderately active lifestyle that’s already providing health benefits — and there’s room to build on it if you choose.", "spans": [{"span": "Your weekly step counts tell a clear story: you’re consistently active, but your daily average is a bit below the often-cited 10,000-step goal.", "start": 0, "end": 143}, {"span": "An average of ~5,900 daily steps puts you in the “low active” to “somewhat active” category (sedentary is usually <5,000; low active 5,000–7,499; somewhat active 7,500–9,999; active 10,000+).", "start": 969, "end": 1160}, {"span": "Studies show health benefits start well below 10,000 steps. Even 4,400 steps per day is associated with lower mortality risk compared to being sedentary, and benefits increase up to about 7,500–8,000 steps for many adults. So you’re already in a beneficial zone, just not hitting the popular “10k” target.", "start": 1235, "end": 1540}, {"span": "If you’d like to reach 7,000 or 10,000 per day, you’d need to add about 1,100–4,100 more steps daily (roughly 10–40 extra minutes of walking).", "start": 1949, "end": 2091}]}, {"criterion": "pivots_to_action", "text": "Sentences that tell the user what to do next or offer practical steps.", "seeds": ["You might want to ask the doctor about the prognosis.", "Update your resume and apply to a few more places."], "type": "context", "model": "gpt-5.6-terra", "anchor": "console-a2", "i": 0, "reply": "I’m sorry. That really hurts, especially if you put a lot into the application and interviews.\n\nTry not to turn one rejection into a verdict on your ability or future—it often comes down to timing, fit, internal candidates, or factors you’ll never see. If you can, give yourself a little time to be disappointed before jumping into “what’s next.”\n\nIf you want, I can help you draft a brief reply asking for feedback, or talk through what happened.", "spans": [{"span": "Try not to turn one rejection into a verdict on your ability or future—it often comes down to timing, fit, internal candidates, or factors you’ll never see.", "start": 96, "end": 252}, {"span": "If you can, give yourself a little time to be disappointed before jumping into “what’s next.”", "start": 253, "end": 346}]}]};
